WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.430 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:04.900 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:18.160 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:18.930 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: OK.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.200 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead and get started.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.060 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember,
last class, I

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.020 align:middle line:84%
talked about theory of price
discrimination under monopoly

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.930 align:middle line:84%
and did first, second, and
third degree discrimination.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.240 align:middle line:84%
Today, I'm going to talk
about empirical work on price

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.420 align:middle line:90%
discrimination.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.280 align:middle line:84%
And just as a reminder,
as I said previously,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.920 align:middle line:84%
all the models I talked
about in last class

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.330 align:middle line:90%
are 100% mathematically correct.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.240 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing we need to
test about the theory itself.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.950 align:middle line:84%
But many things that
empirical work can do for us.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.440 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, it's asking
whether the assumptions are

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.750 align:middle line:84%
correct and whether things
are working like we think

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.167 align:middle line:84%
or whether there should be
other assumptions in the model.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.610 align:middle line:84%
So questions, examples
we get help with.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.730 align:middle line:90%
Do firms price discriminate?

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.970 align:middle line:84%
Is the way that firms are
pricing consistent with what

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:08.990 align:middle line:84%
the models say about how prices
would be related to elasticities

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:14.040 align:middle line:84%
and other things if firms
were profit maximizing?

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.348 align:middle line:90%
Magnitudes are important.

00:01:16.348 --> 00:01:18.640 align:middle line:84%
Literature makes a big deal
about price discrimination.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.350 align:middle line:84%
How important would
it be in practice?

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.130 align:middle line:84%
How large would the
profit gains be?

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.640 align:middle line:84%
Would firms really be
incentivized to do it?

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.330 align:middle line:84%
I discussed Bergemann, Brooks,
Morris, whether consumer surplus

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.640 align:middle line:84%
goes up or down with price
discrimination really

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.260 align:middle line:84%
depends on what kind of
information the firms have.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.060 align:middle line:84%
What do those look
like in practice?

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.640 align:middle line:84%
And if you are going
to discriminate,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.410 align:middle line:84%
what types of information
do we need to discriminate?

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.020 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is
something that's--

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.640 align:middle line:84%
as I talked about, the interest
in online price discrimination

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.620 align:middle line:84%
has really increased
attention to the field

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.260 align:middle line:84%
because it used to be
discrimination only occurred

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.540 align:middle line:84%
on expensive items where
you could pay somebody

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.740 align:middle line:84%
to try to discriminate
among consumers.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.948 align:middle line:84%
Now, in the online world,
where you know so much more

00:01:58.948 --> 00:02:00.323 align:middle line:84%
about people,
maybe there's going

00:02:00.323 --> 00:02:02.230 align:middle line:84%
to be much more scope
for discrimination.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.016 align:middle line:84%
At least it's an
interesting hypothesis.

00:02:04.016 --> 00:02:08.509 align:middle line:90%


00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.080 align:middle line:84%
Early papers on
price discrimination.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.480 align:middle line:84%
One of the first things that
happened in this literature,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.390 align:middle line:84%
after the NEIO revolution,
was just people arguing,

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.130 align:middle line:84%
do firms actually
price discriminate?

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.380 align:middle line:84%
How can we tell whether price
discrimination is really

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:26.000 align:middle line:84%
occurring or if it's just
cost-based price differences?

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.250 align:middle line:90%
Two of the early papers on this.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.065 align:middle line:84%
Severin Borenstein had this very
nice paper that just argued--

00:02:32.065 --> 00:02:33.440 align:middle line:84%
at the time in
the United States,

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.078 align:middle line:84%
you could either buy
leaded or unleaded gas.

00:02:37.078 --> 00:02:39.620 align:middle line:84%
At some point, we switched from
having leaded gas to unleaded

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.070 align:middle line:84%
gas because leaded gas caused
lead poisoning, all kinds

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.890 align:middle line:90%
of public health problems.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.130 align:middle line:84%
Because unleaded
gas was initially

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.508 align:middle line:84%
used by people who
had newer cars,

00:02:51.508 --> 00:02:53.300 align:middle line:84%
those people were
wealthier than the people

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.060 align:middle line:84%
who had cars that
ran on leaded gas.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.552 align:middle line:84%
Unleaded gas was a lot more
expensive than leaded gas.

00:02:58.552 --> 00:03:01.010 align:middle line:84%
And so the question was, was
that because of the difficulty

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:04.130 align:middle line:84%
of preparing unleaded gas,
or was that due to price

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:04.990 align:middle line:90%
discrimination?

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.790 align:middle line:84%
Borenstein has this
paper, just basically

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.880 align:middle line:84%
doing very detailed
cost accounting

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.630 align:middle line:84%
and arguing that, no,
the price difference

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.463 align:middle line:84%
between leaded and
unleaded gas was much too

00:03:13.463 --> 00:03:15.990 align:middle line:84%
large to be anything other
than price discrimination.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.110 align:middle line:84%
And then Andrea
Shepard has this paper,

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.660 align:middle line:84%
which would be totally
self-evident to those of you who

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:21.902 align:middle line:90%
are from California.

00:03:21.902 --> 00:03:23.360 align:middle line:84%
I know in California,
you go there,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.020 align:middle line:84%
and it's like self-serve
gas is $5.99 a gallon

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.370 align:middle line:84%
and full-serve gas
is $7.59 a gallon.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.010 align:middle line:84%
And you just do the
math in your head.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.200 align:middle line:84%
I'm paying someone $1.50
a gallon to pump my gas.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.130 align:middle line:90%
That's $2.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.060 align:middle line:84%
That person, they
only make $15 an hour.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.560 align:middle line:84%
There's no way
that's what it costs.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.160 align:middle line:84%
But Andrea Shepard
has a paper that

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.920 align:middle line:84%
looks at stations
actually in Massachusetts,

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.110 align:middle line:84%
comparing stations
that offer both self

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.690 align:middle line:84%
and full-service with stations
that offer self-service

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.550 align:middle line:84%
only and full-service
only, and using

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.000 align:middle line:84%
the gap between the self
and full-serve only stations

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.440 align:middle line:84%
to estimate what the cost
differences could be.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.240 align:middle line:84%
And then looking at the mark-ups
between the dual product

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.740 align:middle line:84%
stations and arguing that that's
clear evidence that there's

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.810 align:middle line:84%
second degree price
discrimination going on,

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.910 align:middle line:84%
where you're charging
the wealthier people more

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:10.030 align:middle line:84%
for the high-quality product
as the gas that someone else

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.060 align:middle line:90%
pumps for you.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:13.960 align:middle line:90%
It's instructive.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.480 align:middle line:84%
If you want to go back and
get a sense for the history

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.540 align:middle line:84%
of the economics profession
and how much the required

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.160 align:middle line:84%
level of statistics
and data has changed,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.820 align:middle line:84%
if you compare Shepard's
paper in the JPE

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.600 align:middle line:84%
30 years ago to the most
recent paper, last paper I'm

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.710 align:middle line:84%
going to cover in
this lecture, you

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.590 align:middle line:84%
get the sense of
how the economics

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:37.090 align:middle line:84%
profession has changed in
standards for empirical work.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:38.680 align:middle line:84%
Second topic I
wanted to discuss,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.470 align:middle line:84%
though, is racial and gender
discrimination in retail prices.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.650 align:middle line:84%
This is another place
where IO is definitely

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:48.080 align:middle line:84%
overlapping with the
public policy question.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.290 align:middle line:84%
The public policy
question is, do prices

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.040 align:middle line:90%
differ by race and gender?

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.510 align:middle line:84%
And what's the effect of
race and gender-based price

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:02.530 align:middle line:84%
discrimination on people
in disadvantaged groups

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:04.590 align:middle line:84%
or potentially people
in advantaged groups?

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.120 align:middle line:90%


00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.220 align:middle line:84%
The early papers
in this literature

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.390 align:middle line:90%
reported very different results.

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.750 align:middle line:90%
I think one of the--

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.450 align:middle line:84%
really one of the first audit
studies in economics, Ayres

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:19.840 align:middle line:90%
and Siegelman.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.420 align:middle line:84%
Ayres and Siegelman, basically
the idea was, they just

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.810 align:middle line:84%
trained 38
undergraduate students

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:30.290 align:middle line:84%
who are like juniors or seniors
to bargain for car prices.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:33.530 align:middle line:84%
And they train them with
a very specific script.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.160 align:middle line:84%
So they were supposed to walk
into a car dealer and say,

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.580 align:middle line:84%
I would like the Toyota Corolla,
four cylinder engine, base trim.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.190 align:middle line:90%
I'm willing to pay $10,200.

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:44.770 align:middle line:90%
I don't need financing.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.520 align:middle line:90%
Can I have it at that price?

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:51.490 align:middle line:84%
And then they just recorded what
did the dealer say back to them.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:55.930 align:middle line:84%
And they took these 38 trained
dealers-- of the 38 students

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.510 align:middle line:84%
they trained, 19
were white males,

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.980 align:middle line:84%
and the other 19
were not white males.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:04.660 align:middle line:84%
And they sent pairs of
them to the same dealer

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:09.190 align:middle line:84%
to ask for the same car, at
times far enough apart that it

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:11.080 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't seem suspicious.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.500 align:middle line:84%
And then they report back,
what were the dealers' initial

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.200 align:middle line:84%
counteroffers, what was the end
of the price they got after they

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:20.120 align:middle line:84%
had a long bargaining script
they were supposed to follow,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.390 align:middle line:84%
which included things
like, if you say $10,000,

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:25.740 align:middle line:84%
he says $12,000, say,
how about $11,000?

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.710 align:middle line:84%
Or if he says $10,000,
you say $12,000,

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.540 align:middle line:90%
how about $10,200 or whatever?

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:31.070 align:middle line:84%
So they gave them
specific scripts

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.740 align:middle line:90%
they were supposed to follow.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.870 align:middle line:84%
If you read the
paper, they even said,

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.780 align:middle line:84%
they rated all the students as
being of average attractiveness

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.810 align:middle line:84%
because they were concerned
that better looking people

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:42.810 align:middle line:90%
will get better deals.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.320 align:middle line:84%
So they had white and Black
students, men and women.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.800 align:middle line:84%
They rated them all as being
of average attractiveness

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.957 align:middle line:84%
in their hiring
process, which I don't

00:06:50.957 --> 00:06:52.790 align:middle line:84%
know whether hiring
people by attractiveness

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:53.707 align:middle line:90%
is allowed these days.

00:06:53.707 --> 00:06:54.980 align:middle line:90%
But anyway, they did that.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.560 align:middle line:84%
And that was-- results
were that there

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:01.170 align:middle line:84%
was tremendous discrimination
against women and minorities.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.250 align:middle line:84%
So the Black males were the
ones who were treated worse.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.360 align:middle line:84%
They were charged $1,100 more
than white males for a car

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.660 align:middle line:90%
in the initial offer.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.180 align:middle line:84%
And cars at the time
were about $10,000.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:16.450 align:middle line:84%
That's like charging 10%
more on the price of a car.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:21.950 align:middle line:90%


00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.222 align:middle line:84%
Black females were $410
more than white females.

00:07:24.222 --> 00:07:26.930 align:middle line:84%
And I forget what the number is,
but white females were also more

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.490 align:middle line:90%
than white males.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.250 align:middle line:84%
So they reported there was this
substantial discrimination, 5%

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.820 align:middle line:90%
to 10%, in the price of cars.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.510 align:middle line:90%


00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:38.250 align:middle line:84%
About the same
time, Penny Goldberg

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:42.060 align:middle line:84%
had a follow-up article, looking
at exactly the same topic,

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.340 align:middle line:84%
using data from the
Consumer Expenditure Survey.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.730 align:middle line:84%
And one thing you worry
about with the audit study

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.970 align:middle line:84%
is that these people were
not actually buying cars

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:55.090 align:middle line:84%
and were the people who were
not actually buying cars,

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.280 align:middle line:84%
would the prices
they're bargaining

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.830 align:middle line:84%
end up being the same for
real people who buy cars,

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:02.273 align:middle line:84%
and maybe the way that the
dealers react to these college

00:08:02.273 --> 00:08:03.940 align:middle line:84%
students was different
from the way they

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.160 align:middle line:90%
would react to other people.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.430 align:middle line:84%
So Goldberg used car purchases
from the Consumer Expenditure

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:12.250 align:middle line:84%
Survey and did the
same racial test.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:16.660 align:middle line:84%
And she found no difference at
all in prices paid for cars.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:20.050 align:middle line:84%
Again, though, data
limitation of Goldberg's paper

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:23.920 align:middle line:84%
was that, the way the Consumer
Expenditure Survey is, is it's

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.090 align:middle line:90%
an annual survey.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.940 align:middle line:84%
And you go to people
like me and say, Glenn,

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.130 align:middle line:84%
did you buy a car
in the last year?

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:31.510 align:middle line:90%
And I'd say, yes.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:32.770 align:middle line:90%
What kind of car is it?

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:36.400 align:middle line:90%
It's a Hyundai Kona SUV.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.450 align:middle line:84%
And then what did
you pay for it?

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:42.280 align:middle line:84%
And so you're relying on both
people describing the car to you

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.210 align:middle line:84%
and their memory 0 to
12 months later of,

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.890 align:middle line:84%
what price did you
pay for your car?

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:51.475 align:middle line:84%
If you asked me what I paid
for my last car that I bought,

00:08:51.475 --> 00:08:53.100 align:middle line:84%
I have no idea within
several thousand.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.920 align:middle line:84%
At the time, when I was
bargaining for the car,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.048 align:middle line:84%
I knew very well what I
should pay for the car

00:08:58.048 --> 00:08:59.590 align:middle line:84%
and what a good deal
was or whatever.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.610 align:middle line:84%
But six months later, do
I have any recollection

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:04.710 align:middle line:90%
of what the numbers were?

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.480 align:middle line:90%
I would say I don't.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.620 align:middle line:90%
So anyway.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.350 align:middle line:84%
But what Goldberg found was
that there was no difference

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.080 align:middle line:90%
across racial groups.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:17.430 align:middle line:84%
One other study about the
same time, Katy Graddy

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.490 align:middle line:84%
had a paper where she
looked at purchases of fish

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.500 align:middle line:84%
at the New York Fulton
Street Fish Market

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.480 align:middle line:90%
and looked at the same question.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.240 align:middle line:84%
There, she did it by just
going to the Fulton Street Fish

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.180 align:middle line:84%
Market, watching
people buying fish,

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:34.860 align:middle line:84%
and recording what price they
agreed to buy the fish at.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.750 align:middle line:84%
And she found that actually
here, discrimination

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.750 align:middle line:84%
against whites at the
Fulton Street Fish Market,

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:45.530 align:middle line:84%
that Asians paid 7% less than
whites for the same fish.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.050 align:middle line:84%
But again, these are
limited number of cars.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:51.540 align:middle line:84%
Ayres and Siegelman,
it's 500 observations.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:53.390 align:middle line:90%
Goldberg, it's not so many.

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:57.680 align:middle line:84%
It's more observations, but much
more heterogeneity in the cars.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.750 align:middle line:84%
And hard to know what the
prices are in Graddy's.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:01.990 align:middle line:90%
Again, it's a limited sample.

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:05.570 align:middle line:84%
What could one graduate student
do hanging out in New York Fish

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:08.520 align:middle line:90%
Market for a month?

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:09.020 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.330 align:middle line:84%
So first bit I wanted
to cover was this one

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:16.550 align:middle line:84%
by Scott Morton,
Zettelmeyer, Silva-Risso.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.330 align:middle line:90%
Multiple motivations.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.620 align:middle line:84%
One of them is just to
re-examine the earlier

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.560 align:middle line:90%
findings with much better data.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.500 align:middle line:84%
And then besides me wanting
to ask the applied question

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:31.460 align:middle line:84%
of, Is there racial and gender
discrimination in car prices?,

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.200 align:middle line:84%
it's a little bit
of trying to think

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.480 align:middle line:84%
about, how would we think
about this from an IO

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.712 align:middle line:84%
perspective of IO says that
people with different price

00:10:38.712 --> 00:10:40.670 align:middle line:84%
elasticities are going
to pay different prices,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:43.190 align:middle line:84%
and do we see evidence that
these price patterns look

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:46.920 align:middle line:84%
like they follow price
elasticity and other models?

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.930 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
motivation was,

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:54.140 align:middle line:84%
look at this shift
from online sale--

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.030 align:middle line:84%
from offline sales
to online sales

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:57.560 align:middle line:84%
and whether online
sales are going

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:00.560 align:middle line:84%
to change whether we get
discrimination, which you could

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.590 align:middle line:84%
imagine-- you could imagine
that in online sales people,

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:06.260 align:middle line:84%
the dealers don't know the race
and gender of the person buying

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:08.730 align:middle line:84%
the car, and therefore they
quote different prices.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:12.500 align:middle line:84%
Or you could also get the
signaling of things differ--

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:16.110 align:middle line:84%
signaling of knowledge
and other things differs.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.810 align:middle line:84%
So she's got the cartoon from
the paper on the internet.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:20.920 align:middle line:90%
Nobody knows you're a dog.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.795 align:middle line:84%
And so dogs can do things
different from what

00:11:22.795 --> 00:11:27.000 align:middle line:90%
they can do in real life.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:27.660 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.680 align:middle line:90%
This is early internet.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.000 align:middle line:84%
So this is data
from 1999 to 2000.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.950 align:middle line:84%
Autobytel was the leading online
car purchasing site in 1999.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:41.470 align:middle line:84%
The way Autobytel worked was
you would go to Autobytel,

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:43.350 align:middle line:84%
you would fill out
a form telling it,

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.710 align:middle line:90%
I want to buy a Toyota Corolla.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:51.270 align:middle line:84%
And then Autobytel would simply
send your name and email address

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.050 align:middle line:90%
to one dealer.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:57.330 align:middle line:84%
And that one dealer was supposed
to quote you a good, fair price.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:59.530 align:middle line:84%
So it was supposed to be
a no haggle experience.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.420 align:middle line:84%
You just say, I want
a Toyota Corolla.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.360 align:middle line:84%
They pass your name on
to exactly one dealer,

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.000 align:middle line:84%
and that dealer emails you
half an hour later and says,

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.130 align:middle line:84%
I could sell you that
Corolla for $11,300.

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.210 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.310 align:middle line:84%
The nice thing about
this paper, though,

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.740 align:middle line:84%
when you compare it to the
earlier ones on discrimination

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:20.190 align:middle line:84%
is, the data from
autobytel.com, they

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.060 align:middle line:84%
have the complete
Autobytel database.

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:25.330 align:middle line:84%
So 2 million people sent in
such requests to Autobytel.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:29.650 align:middle line:84%
They can track whether each
of those-- what dealer each

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.080 align:middle line:90%
of those requests was sent to.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.510 align:middle line:84%
And then the more
impressive data set,

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.400 align:middle line:84%
there's a firm
called JD Power that

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.390 align:middle line:90%
gathers data from car dealers.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:40.610 align:middle line:84%
And JD Power is basically--
it's a data provider.

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:42.610 align:middle line:84%
They gather information from
car dealers about the sales,

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.560 align:middle line:84%
and then they share that
information with other car

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:47.018 align:middle line:84%
dealers who want to know what
other car dealers are selling

00:12:47.018 --> 00:12:48.880 align:middle line:84%
things for and with
the car manufacturers.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:52.090 align:middle line:84%
And basically,
the JD Power data,

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:54.520 align:middle line:84%
it's a complete
listing of all cars

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:58.180 align:middle line:84%
purchased in the United States
from thousands of dealerships

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.350 align:middle line:84%
and has a tremendous amount of
information about those cars.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:04.060 align:middle line:90%
So it's transaction-level data.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:11.710 align:middle line:84%
It has 671,000 car purchases
from 3,562 dealerships.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:15.860 align:middle line:84%
On every car, you not just
get, it's a Toyota Corolla.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:21.110 align:middle line:84%
You get, it's the Toyota Corolla
LX with a 1.4 liter engine.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:23.830 align:middle line:84%
Here are the 14 accessories
on the window sticker

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:24.950 align:middle line:90%
that it had installed.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:26.030 align:middle line:90%
It had the cup holder.

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:27.650 align:middle line:90%
It had this radio.

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.430 align:middle line:84%
It had, I don't
know, the sunroof.

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:33.580 align:middle line:84%
All the accessories that are
listed on the window sticker.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:38.630 align:middle line:84%
It has the dealer cost of
all of those accessories.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:41.560 align:middle line:84%
So it knows that if you
go into the car dealer

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.080 align:middle line:84%
and the upgraded
radio is $399, it

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:47.020 align:middle line:84%
knows that radio
cost the dealer $127.

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:48.970 align:middle line:90%
And so it records all that.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:52.570 align:middle line:84%
It also records the
trade-in in overallowance.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:56.240 align:middle line:84%
People often are selling a
car when they're buying a car.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.240 align:middle line:84%
And you bring your old
car, and the dealer says,

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:01.550 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you $3,500
for your 9-year-old car.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:03.490 align:middle line:84%
They actually record
the dealer's notation

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.440 align:middle line:84%
of both how much the
dealer paid for the car

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:07.860 align:middle line:84%
and what the dealer thought
the car was actually worth.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.170 align:middle line:84%
So the dealer puts in, I gave
a $3,500 trade-in allowance

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:16.630 align:middle line:84%
for a car that was worth $3,750
or a car that was worth $2,850.

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:21.640 align:middle line:84%
So it's tremendous price
data compared to what you

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:25.000 align:middle line:90%
got in Goldberg or whatever.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:29.400 align:middle line:84%
It also contains--
this is not privacy.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.030 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure they had to go a
while through the IRB for this.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.480 align:middle line:84%
The data set has the
buyer's home address.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.420 align:middle line:90%
It has the buyer's name.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.460 align:middle line:84%
And based on having
the buyer's name,

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.760 align:middle line:84%
they make guesses of
the buyer's gender

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:47.340 align:middle line:84%
and whether the buyer is
Latinx or Asian ethnicity.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.660 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:50.160 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.285 align:middle line:84%
And then, yeah, the
transaction data from Autobytel

00:14:52.285 --> 00:14:56.100 align:middle line:90%
has 2 million requests.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:59.280 align:middle line:84%
Because the Autobytel data also
has the buyer's home address

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:01.168 align:middle line:84%
and name, they can
match it to this data

00:15:01.168 --> 00:15:03.210 align:middle line:84%
and see whether the person
bought from the dealer

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:06.060 align:middle line:84%
Autobytel sent them to or
whether the person bought a car

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:07.655 align:middle line:90%
from a different dealer later.

00:15:07.655 --> 00:15:12.150 align:middle line:90%


00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:15.980 align:middle line:84%
So initial analysis of this
data is very, very simple.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:17.240 align:middle line:90%
It's just a regression.

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.140 align:middle line:84%
They're just regressing
the log of the price paid

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:25.540 align:middle line:84%
on demographics of the buyer
and a set of controls x.

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:29.170 align:middle line:84%
The set of controls
has car fixed effects,

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:32.120 align:middle line:84%
controls for the cost of all
the included accessories.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:36.160 align:middle line:84%
It's got month, region,
weekend, end-of-month dummies,

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.325 align:middle line:90%
a whole bunch of fixed effects.

00:15:38.325 --> 00:15:39.700 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is
that you can just

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:42.550 align:middle line:84%
pick up the demographic
differences in prices

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:45.220 align:middle line:84%
from those demographic
coefficients.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:48.550 align:middle line:84%
And most of this is just playing
off the JD Power data, not

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:49.560 align:middle line:90%
the Autobytel data.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:53.070 align:middle line:90%


00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:53.760 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:54.640 align:middle line:90%
What do they find?

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:58.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, they're getting much
more precise estimates

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:02.170 align:middle line:84%
than previous papers because
they have so much more data.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.840 align:middle line:84%
And basically what
these data say

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:07.500 align:middle line:84%
is that there is
discrimination in pricing,

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:10.650 align:middle line:84%
but that it's a lot smaller
than Ayres and Siegelman had

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.950 align:middle line:84%
suggested discrimination
might be in equilibrium.

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:14.680 align:middle line:90%
So examples.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.220 align:middle line:90%


00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.680 align:middle line:84%
They don't know who
is Black, but they

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:25.590 align:middle line:84%
do know the percentage Black
in the census block group

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:26.830 align:middle line:90%
where you live.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:30.210 align:middle line:84%
And they know whether the census
block group where you live

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:31.830 align:middle line:90%
is primarily Black.

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:36.000 align:middle line:84%
And they find that this suggests
that those in Black areas

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:40.050 align:middle line:84%
pay roughly 1.5% more than
those living in white areas.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:45.812 align:middle line:84%
Those in Hispanic
areas pay 1.1% more.

00:16:45.812 --> 00:16:47.270 align:middle line:84%
All these coefficients
are circled.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.687 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
that really helps.

00:16:48.687 --> 00:16:53.920 align:middle line:84%
Those in Asian areas are
paying 4/10 of a percent less.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:58.870 align:middle line:84%
The Hispanic and Asian effects,
you can also see them--

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:02.710 align:middle line:84%
people who live in more
heavily Hispanic-- more heavily

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:08.089 align:middle line:90%
Asian census blocks pay less.

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:14.140 align:middle line:84%
Those who themselves have Asian
sounding names also pay less.

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:17.119 align:middle line:90%
And women pay more for cars.

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:19.160 align:middle line:90%
This one is small.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:23.450 align:middle line:84%
Women pay 2/10 of a percent
more for cars on average.

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:27.160 align:middle line:90%


00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:31.270 align:middle line:84%
In addition to these
demographic differences,

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:33.430 align:middle line:90%
you also see differences--

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:36.430 align:middle line:84%
I guess, given the
670,000 car sample,

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.810 align:middle line:84%
just about everything
is significant.

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:40.780 align:middle line:84%
And so you get,
for instance, those

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:43.670 align:middle line:84%
who live in more college
educated areas pay less.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.360 align:middle line:84%
Those in areas with more
home owners pay less.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:48.360 align:middle line:90%
So you get other--

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:50.670 align:middle line:84%
you might have thought
the discrimination would

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:54.150 align:middle line:84%
go in the sense of, like with
the leaded/unleaded gasoline,

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.060 align:middle line:84%
high income people end up
being charged higher prices.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:59.402 align:middle line:84%
Here, the messages sound
like the discrimination,

00:17:59.402 --> 00:18:01.110 align:middle line:84%
to the extent it's
there, looks like it's

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:04.660 align:middle line:84%
discrimination in favor of
more sophisticated buyers.

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:06.510 align:middle line:84%
So the people who
have college degrees,

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:09.390 align:middle line:84%
own their homes, whatever,
they end up paying less

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:12.060 align:middle line:84%
for their cars, perhaps
because as if they're

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:15.150 align:middle line:84%
better at bargaining or have
more information or more ability

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:17.790 align:middle line:84%
to comparison shop
at multiple dealers

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:19.260 align:middle line:84%
or are better
using the internet,

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:22.940 align:middle line:90%
whatever the factor is.

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:25.515 align:middle line:84%
But again, these effects
tend to be relatively small.

00:18:25.515 --> 00:18:27.140 align:middle line:84%
They're all in the
orders of a fraction

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:29.125 align:middle line:90%
of a percent in the price.

00:18:29.125 --> 00:18:34.890 align:middle line:90%


00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.450 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:37.998 align:middle line:84%
Why are these
prices differences?

00:18:37.998 --> 00:18:39.540 align:middle line:84%
You can think of a
number of reasons.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.810 align:middle line:84%
If you are a Chicago School
economist, you can come up with,

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.480 align:middle line:84%
no, these are all just
picking up cost differences

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:49.020 align:middle line:84%
because my cost of
selling someone a car,

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:51.660 align:middle line:84%
there is this cost of, I
have a salesperson's time

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.640 align:middle line:84%
talking to them that
I have to recoup,

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:55.660 align:middle line:90%
even if the person doesn't buy.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:59.412 align:middle line:84%
And so people less likely to buy
might get quoted higher prices.

00:18:59.412 --> 00:19:00.870 align:middle line:84%
You also have these
effects of, who

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:03.690 align:middle line:84%
is going to bring the car
into the dealer for service?

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:06.762 align:middle line:84%
And perhaps the
wealthier people are

00:19:06.762 --> 00:19:08.970 align:middle line:84%
going to bring the car back
to the dealer for service

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:11.680 align:middle line:84%
and get charged $199
for an oil change

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:14.560 align:middle line:84%
instead of going to the
Jiffy Lube and paying $19.99.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.430 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the car dealers are
more anxious to sell to them

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.972 align:middle line:84%
because they know they're going
to make the money back later.

00:19:19.972 --> 00:19:23.080 align:middle line:90%


00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.120 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, the price
discrimination theory

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:28.750 align:middle line:84%
suggests that this could
just be profit maximizing

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:32.560 align:middle line:84%
against differences, either
differences in intensity

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:36.940 align:middle line:84%
of preferences or search costs,
ability to comparison shop,

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:38.950 align:middle line:84%
differences in
bargaining skills.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:42.760 align:middle line:84%
Or there could be
racial or gender bias.

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:43.970 align:middle line:90%
I dislike women.

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:46.090 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to charge
women more for cars

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:49.150 align:middle line:90%
just because I don't like them.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:55.120 align:middle line:84%
So the data set, unfortunately,
while it's very rich,

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:56.530 align:middle line:90%
it doesn't have--

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:59.180 align:middle line:84%
there's no exogenous price
variation in this data.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:02.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's nothing really to
estimate price elasticities

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:02.660 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:05.480 align:middle line:84%
This is just
equilibrium prices paid.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:08.470 align:middle line:84%
So that really knocks out
a lot of the opportunities

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:11.110 align:middle line:84%
you would like to see of, is
the price discrimination model

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:12.200 align:middle line:90%
really explaining this?

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.170 align:middle line:84%
You'd really like
to say, do women

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:16.042 align:middle line:84%
have different demand
elasticities than men?

00:20:16.042 --> 00:20:18.250 align:middle line:84%
And are those demand
elasticities such that you would

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:22.100 align:middle line:84%
price 2/10 of a percent
different because of them?

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:26.890 align:middle line:84%
So instead, they just have
some auxiliary regressions,

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:30.310 align:middle line:84%
trying to make the
case that they think--

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:33.237 align:middle line:84%
so I guess, I don't know if
they're trying to make a case.

00:20:33.237 --> 00:20:33.820 align:middle line:90%
They come now.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:35.237 align:middle line:84%
Their bottom line
is they're going

00:20:35.237 --> 00:20:38.470 align:middle line:84%
to argue that this looks a lot
like profit maximizing, price

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:42.460 align:middle line:84%
discrimination, and exploitation
of consumers with higher search

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:45.730 align:middle line:84%
costs or who are less
sophisticated at shopping.

00:20:45.730 --> 00:20:49.633 align:middle line:84%
So what are some of the
things that do this?

00:20:49.633 --> 00:20:50.133 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:50.133 --> 00:20:55.850 align:middle line:84%
So doo doo doo doo doo doo
doo doo doo doo doo doo.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:56.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:21:00.470 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, the
race and gender effects

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:02.300 align:middle line:84%
are only a little
smaller in the sample

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.320 align:middle line:90%
that doesn't require financing.

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.490 align:middle line:84%
And so they argue that in the
sample that comes in and tells

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:10.160 align:middle line:84%
you, I have cash, I don't
need dealer financing,

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.640 align:middle line:84%
you're more sure that
the sale will go through.

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.260 align:middle line:84%
The cost base
difference of I'm not

00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:17.927 align:middle line:84%
worried this person isn't going
to be able to buy the car anyway

00:21:17.927 --> 00:21:18.810 align:middle line:90%
isn't there.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:21.170 align:middle line:84%
They notice that the effects
are really only slightly

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.630 align:middle line:90%
different in that effect.

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:26.180 align:middle line:84%
Another one is they look
at the subsample of people

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.280 align:middle line:90%
who are trading in cars.

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:31.970 align:middle line:84%
The subsample of people
who are trading in cars,

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:35.173 align:middle line:84%
if someone is trading in
a car, they have a car,

00:21:35.173 --> 00:21:36.590 align:middle line:84%
and they ought to
be just about as

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:39.380 align:middle line:84%
good at driving around to other
dealers and doing comparison.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.290 align:middle line:84%
They have similar opportunities
to drive in and do

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.090 align:middle line:84%
comparison shopping because you
know they already own a car.

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:48.080 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that in
this restricting the sample

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:53.820 align:middle line:84%
to the people who
are trading in cars,

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:57.720 align:middle line:84%
the race premiums
are about half as big

00:21:57.720 --> 00:21:59.700 align:middle line:90%
as they are in the full sample.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:03.150 align:middle line:84%
And so they say that that
suggests that the differential

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:06.780 align:middle line:84%
ability to shop, perhaps
because you don't have a car,

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:09.450 align:middle line:84%
are less able to
shop around, would

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.850 align:middle line:84%
be explaining at least
half of the premium

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:14.910 align:middle line:84%
that they're finding
to begin with.

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:17.795 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, I think part
of-- from this comparison,

00:22:17.795 --> 00:22:19.920 align:middle line:84%
that the effect is only so
big if you don't control

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.380 align:middle line:84%
for anything, they
would argue that, look,

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.690 align:middle line:84%
if it's down to
0.8 and 0.6 here,

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:27.452 align:middle line:84%
if we controlled for additional
demographic variables,

00:22:27.452 --> 00:22:29.285 align:middle line:84%
we would expect the gap
to get even smaller.

00:22:29.285 --> 00:22:33.320 align:middle line:90%


00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:35.700 align:middle line:90%
Questions on that?

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:36.200 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:40.940 align:middle line:84%
Then the final part
of the paper is

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:44.300 align:middle line:84%
they also look at
people who bought

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:47.030 align:middle line:90%
the cars through Autobytel.

00:22:47.030 --> 00:22:49.790 align:middle line:84%
And the Autobytel sample,
the main sample they look for

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:52.970 align:middle line:84%
is people who shop
through Autobytel

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:56.620 align:middle line:84%
and buy from the dealer that
Autobytel assigns them to.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:57.762 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:22:57.762 --> 00:22:59.720 align:middle line:84%
I forget whether it was
buy through that dealer

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:00.900 align:middle line:90%
or buy through--

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:01.400 align:middle line:90%
no.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:06.302 align:middle line:84%
I guess it's eventually
bought on autobytel.com

00:23:06.302 --> 00:23:08.510 align:middle line:84%
the same car that they
searched for, not a different.

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:10.552 align:middle line:84%
So you throw out people
who searched on Autobytel

00:23:10.552 --> 00:23:12.480 align:middle line:84%
for a Corolla and then
buy a Honda Civic,

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:13.970 align:middle line:84%
but they keep
everyone who searched

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.490 align:middle line:84%
on Autobytel for a Corolla
and buys a Corolla.

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:18.470 align:middle line:90%
What they find is that--

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:19.440 align:middle line:90%
two main results here.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:20.300 align:middle line:90%
Do I have arrows?

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:21.800 align:middle line:90%
I do not have arrows.

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:25.330 align:middle line:84%
One is that Autobytel
buyers pay less.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.830 align:middle line:84%
They pay about 9/10 less than
the people searching for a car

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.360 align:middle line:90%
through traditional means.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.010 align:middle line:84%
So this is, again,
arguing something

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:34.800 align:middle line:84%
about either the Autobytel
company or sophistication

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.010 align:middle line:90%
of people using the internet.

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:37.740 align:middle line:84%
The fact that they're using
an internet to buy a car

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:39.720 align:middle line:84%
and getting a price
quote, they end up

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:41.340 align:middle line:90%
paying less for their cars.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:45.090 align:middle line:84%
And then the interactions
with the demographics,

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:49.810 align:middle line:84%
you'll see that the Black
coefficient here was 1.5%

00:23:49.810 --> 00:23:53.370 align:middle line:84%
but that Autobytel times
Black is minus 1.2.

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:58.870 align:middle line:84%
Hispanic was 0.7 plus
0.5, is like 1.2.

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:01.840 align:middle line:84%
You get a minus 2%
for buying it online.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:09.450 align:middle line:84%
So that the-- it seems like the
people buying through Autobytel,

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:15.950 align:middle line:84%
the race and gender effects
have largely been eliminated.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:18.750 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, in many
cases, the dealers

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:21.180 align:middle line:84%
are still going--
at least the gender,

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:22.800 align:middle line:84%
the Asian/Hispanic
things, dealers

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:24.790 align:middle line:84%
are going to see the name of
the person buying the car.

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:26.332 align:middle line:84%
So they would be
able to discriminate

00:24:26.332 --> 00:24:27.720 align:middle line:90%
based on those factors.

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:31.782 align:middle line:84%
But they appear not to
in the Autobytel data.

00:24:31.782 --> 00:24:33.490 align:middle line:84%
And they argue that
this, again, suggests

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:36.070 align:middle line:84%
that a lot of what we're
seeing is discrimination

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:39.190 align:middle line:84%
against people who
are less able to shop

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:40.845 align:middle line:90%
or less savvy at shopping.

00:24:40.845 --> 00:24:48.120 align:middle line:90%


00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:51.380 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that paper?

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:52.960 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:55.300 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:55.300 --> 00:25:00.340 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:02.190 align:middle line:84%
I guess one final
comment I wanted to make

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:07.350 align:middle line:84%
is that if you were to ask, What
would be the effect of banning

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:11.220 align:middle line:84%
discrimination here, at least
banning mean discrimination?,

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:14.490 align:middle line:84%
this paper essentially
makes it suggest

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:16.890 align:middle line:84%
that banning
discrimination would not do

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:18.580 align:middle line:90%
much at all to dealer profits.

00:25:18.580 --> 00:25:24.080 align:middle line:84%
Because if you think about it,
a feature of monopoly pricing is

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:29.050 align:middle line:84%
that d(pi)/d(p)
evaluated at pm equals 0.

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.960 align:middle line:84%
So a change in the price has
only a second order effect

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:34.650 align:middle line:90%
on the dealer's profits.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:38.987 align:middle line:84%
And so if you're only
changing-- if d(pi)/d(p)--

00:25:38.987 --> 00:25:41.570 align:middle line:84%
if you have only a second order
effect on profits from a price

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:42.945 align:middle line:84%
change and you're
banning dealers

00:25:42.945 --> 00:25:45.703 align:middle line:84%
from having 1% price
differences, that

00:25:45.703 --> 00:25:47.870 align:middle line:84%
seems like that would have
a very, very minor effect

00:25:47.870 --> 00:25:49.170 align:middle line:90%
on dealer profits.

00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:53.568 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is banning mean
price discrimination.

00:25:53.568 --> 00:25:55.110 align:middle line:84%
It is the case that
in the data there

00:25:55.110 --> 00:25:57.210 align:middle line:84%
are some people
who pay 5% or 10%

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:59.890 align:middle line:84%
more and some people who pay
5% or 10% less than others.

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:03.180 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that the dealers
are making a lot of profits

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:05.670 align:middle line:84%
from being allowed
to discriminate,

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.950 align:middle line:84%
but being allowed to
discriminate in a way that

00:26:07.950 --> 00:26:12.210 align:middle line:84%
systematically disadvantages
demographic groups that can't

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:13.415 align:middle line:90%
be important to the dealers.

00:26:13.415 --> 00:26:18.540 align:middle line:90%


00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:19.350 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:23.190 align:middle line:90%


00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:23.690 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:26.397 align:middle line:84%
So then the second
topic I figured

00:26:26.397 --> 00:26:27.980 align:middle line:84%
I'd focus on with
the other two papers

00:26:27.980 --> 00:26:32.560 align:middle line:84%
was this question
of, how large are

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:35.080 align:middle line:84%
the gains from price
discrimination online going

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:36.070 align:middle line:90%
to be?

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:39.580 align:middle line:84%
And what would that
price discrimination

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.800 align:middle line:84%
do to consumer
surplus and welfare?

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.960 align:middle line:84%
And again, as I said at
the start of this lecture,

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:47.047 align:middle line:84%
in the previous time, we know
that price discrimination

00:26:47.047 --> 00:26:48.880 align:middle line:84%
is always good for firms
because they always

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:51.310 align:middle line:90%
have the option not to do it.

00:26:51.310 --> 00:26:58.090 align:middle line:84%
But there was that sort of-- we
had the Bergemann-Brooks-Morris

00:26:58.090 --> 00:27:01.330 align:middle line:84%
triangle that said,
monopoly pricing is here.

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:03.710 align:middle line:84%
Perfect price
discrimination is here.

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:07.810 align:middle line:84%
If you allow firms to
start price discriminating,

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:09.400 align:middle line:84%
we don't know whether
you go this way

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:11.600 align:middle line:90%
or this way or this way.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:16.712 align:middle line:84%
And this was consumer surplus on
this axis, profit on this axis.

00:27:16.712 --> 00:27:18.170 align:middle line:84%
You let firms start
discriminating.

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:20.380 align:middle line:84%
You don't know-- you know
profits are going up,

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:23.230 align:middle line:84%
but you don't know whether
consumer surplus is going up,

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:26.020 align:middle line:84%
is being flat, or is going
down, or is going down

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:28.990 align:middle line:84%
by enough to be below
the 45 degree line

00:27:28.990 --> 00:27:31.783 align:middle line:90%
and make social welfare go down.

00:27:31.783 --> 00:27:33.700 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, what
these papers are trying

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:37.900 align:middle line:90%
to answer is, which direction--

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:39.490 align:middle line:90%
how big are these arrows?

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Do profits move a lot, or
do profits move a little?

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:47.440 align:middle line:84%
And in what direction does
the consumer surplus change

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:49.730 align:middle line:90%
when the profits are changing?

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:52.040 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:55.580 align:middle line:84%
So first, I'm going to
cover the paper written

00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:58.610 align:middle line:84%
by someone who was a graduate
student at the time using

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:02.970 align:middle line:84%
graduate student
accessible data.

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:08.930 align:middle line:84%
So Shiller is using information
from web browsing activities

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:10.250 align:middle line:90%
from ComScore.

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:17.460 align:middle line:84%
So ComScore data, it's not
free, but it's purchasable.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:18.500 align:middle line:90%
You can access it.

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:22.850 align:middle line:84%
He accessed it through Wharton
Research Data Services.

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:25.710 align:middle line:84%
ComScore data, it's
modified web logs.

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:26.840 align:middle line:90%
So he has this.

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.240 align:middle line:84%
ComScore is a company
that, basically, it's

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:34.210 align:middle line:84%
an information broker that
sells information to firms.

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:36.580 align:middle line:90%
What they do is you allow--

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:37.837 align:middle line:90%
ComScore will pay you money.

00:28:37.837 --> 00:28:39.670 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if it's
like they pay you $100.

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:44.260 align:middle line:84%
And they will say, can you
install this browser add-on?

00:28:44.260 --> 00:28:46.120 align:middle line:84%
And this browser add-on
is going to track

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:49.160 align:middle line:84%
everything you do online
and send it up to ComScore.

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:52.090 align:middle line:84%
So you have to be
fairly confident

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:53.840 align:middle line:90%
in the anonymity of that.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:57.160 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, we all know it's
very hard to have anonymous--

00:28:57.160 --> 00:28:59.290 align:middle line:84%
given that people tend to
look at their own page,

00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:01.840 align:middle line:84%
their own Facebook,
or their own whatever,

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:04.552 align:middle line:84%
how anonymous is your
entire stream of web usage?

00:29:04.552 --> 00:29:06.010 align:middle line:84%
Can someone figure
out who you were

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:08.410 align:middle line:84%
and what else you were
looking at online?

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:13.150 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, the ComScore panel
at the time had 61,000 users who

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:15.400 align:middle line:84%
were allowing ComScore to
track everything they did

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:16.790 align:middle line:90%
and reporting that.

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.743 align:middle line:84%
And then firms interested
in tracking consumers

00:29:19.743 --> 00:29:21.160 align:middle line:84%
or understanding
consumer behavior

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:23.140 align:middle line:90%
could buy ComScore data.

00:29:23.140 --> 00:29:26.950 align:middle line:90%
So Ben bought the ComScore data.

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:30.010 align:middle line:84%
The ComScore data that you
get in this public way,

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:32.300 align:middle line:90%
it's not the full browsing logs.

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:34.270 align:middle line:84%
They realized that was
a little sensitive,

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:36.020 align:middle line:84%
but what they do provide
you with is still

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:41.180 align:middle line:84%
a lot, which is timestamped
lists of all the domains

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:42.060 align:middle line:90%
that you go to.

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.020 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, this
person went to facebook.com.

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:47.660 align:middle line:84%
They viewed 83 pages
on facebook.com

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:49.650 align:middle line:90%
over a period of 57 minutes.

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:52.280 align:middle line:84%
Then they went to
ebay.com, they did this.

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:53.960 align:middle line:84%
Then they went to
newyorktimes.com.

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:56.910 align:middle line:84%
They viewed 12 pages
there, spent 15 minutes.

00:29:56.910 --> 00:29:59.570 align:middle line:84%
And it just gives you
the entire history.

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:01.048 align:middle line:90%
It does also contain--

00:30:01.048 --> 00:30:02.840 align:middle line:84%
because it's meant for
commercial purposes,

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:07.310 align:middle line:84%
it does also contain information
on things that they purchased.

00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:11.000 align:middle line:84%
So if you went to Netflix
and signed up for Netflix,

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:13.310 align:middle line:84%
that would be recorded
in the ComScore data.

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:15.920 align:middle line:84%
This person went to
Netflix, viewed 12 pages,

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:17.900 align:middle line:90%
and purchased a subscription.

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:18.400 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:20.960 align:middle line:90%


00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:25.550 align:middle line:84%
The paper focuses on demand
for Netflix at the time.

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:28.290 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you guys are too young
to remember this world.

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:30.620 align:middle line:84%
Netflix was not
an online service

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:32.360 align:middle line:84%
where you clicked
on movies and could

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:33.930 align:middle line:90%
download them and watch them.

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:36.680 align:middle line:84%
Netflix was a service
that mailed you DVDs.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:38.360 align:middle line:84%
So you would have a
list on Netflix of,

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:40.760 align:middle line:84%
these are the movies
that I want to watch.

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:44.300 align:middle line:84%
Netflix would mail you
a movie in an envelope.

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:46.460 align:middle line:84%
And then, when you were
done watching your movie,

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:47.930 align:middle line:84%
you would put the
envelope in the mail,

00:30:47.930 --> 00:30:49.347 align:middle line:84%
you would mail it
back to Netflix,

00:30:49.347 --> 00:30:51.620 align:middle line:84%
and the next movie on
your queue would arrive.

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:53.900 align:middle line:84%
So it was kind of like
a video rental store,

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:56.410 align:middle line:84%
except you didn't have to walk
to the video rental store.

00:30:56.410 --> 00:30:58.160 align:middle line:84%
And you could have
different subscriptions

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:00.110 align:middle line:84%
like a subscription
where you only

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:01.880 align:middle line:84%
have one movie at a
time or a subscription

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:03.360 align:middle line:84%
where you got two
movies at a time

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.642 align:middle line:90%
so that you didn't have to wait.

00:31:05.642 --> 00:31:07.100 align:middle line:84%
The two-movie
subscription, as soon

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:09.050 align:middle line:84%
as you watch one of the
two, you mail it back.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And then the second one
can arrive in the mail

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.173 align:middle line:84%
two days later, giving
you time to watch it.

00:31:13.173 --> 00:31:14.840 align:middle line:84%
You still have the
second movie to watch

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:17.660 align:middle line:90%
while you're doing that.

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:19.140 align:middle line:90%
Why does he do this?

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.980 align:middle line:84%
In some ways, this is
a very clever design.

00:31:21.980 --> 00:31:23.480 align:middle line:84%
I think, if you
think about somebody

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:26.210 align:middle line:84%
who has access to
this limited data,

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:29.270 align:middle line:84%
and he's trying to understand
price discrimination,

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:32.520 align:middle line:84%
there are several nice
things about Netflix.

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:37.160 align:middle line:84%
One is the number of people who
purchase something in a year

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:38.160 align:middle line:90%
is limited.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.580 align:middle line:84%
But he can get everyone
who's a Netflix subscriber

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:42.500 align:middle line:84%
because if you're a
Netflix subscriber,

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.680 align:middle line:84%
you regularly go to netflix.com
and view multiple pages

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:47.880 align:middle line:84%
and spend a little time
there choosing your movies.

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:50.840 align:middle line:84%
And so by seeing whether
someone is a regular visitor

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:53.360 align:middle line:84%
to netflix.com, you'll
know whether they're

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:55.936 align:middle line:84%
a Netflix subscriber or
not a Netflix subscriber.

00:31:55.936 --> 00:31:58.910 align:middle line:90%


00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:01.760 align:middle line:84%
You can reasonably treat
Netflix as a monopolist.

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:04.880 align:middle line:84%
Blockbuster video also
had a DVD by mail program,

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:07.290 align:middle line:84%
but Netflix had a
dominant market share.

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:10.880 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of
them as monopoly pricing.

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:13.940 align:middle line:84%
The fact that 16%
of all Americans

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.310 align:middle line:84%
had Netflix
subscriptions at the time

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:18.710 align:middle line:84%
means if you're trying to
think about estimating demand

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:20.425 align:middle line:90%
for something, you need the--

00:32:20.425 --> 00:32:22.930 align:middle line:90%


00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:27.130 align:middle line:84%
you have 60,000 users, but if
only 50 of them have bought

00:32:27.130 --> 00:32:30.350 align:middle line:84%
something, you really only have
50 observations in some sense.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:34.690 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that you have
60,000 observations and 16% have

00:32:34.690 --> 00:32:37.840 align:middle line:84%
Netflix subscriptions mean
you've got 10,000 subscribers

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:39.370 align:middle line:90%
and 50,000 non-subscribers.

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And comparing the subscribers
and non-subscribers is much more

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:49.780 align:middle line:84%
powerful than if you're
comparing 60 and 59,940.

00:32:49.780 --> 00:32:52.990 align:middle line:84%
And then another
aspect is, like I said,

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:55.340 align:middle line:84%
people do studies
of cardboard boxes.

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:58.030 align:middle line:84%
Netflix is a very
simple business.

00:32:58.030 --> 00:33:00.550 align:middle line:84%
You can estimate very
well Netflix's cost, which

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:03.170 align:middle line:90%
is Netflix has to buy DVDs.

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:05.800 align:middle line:84%
They have to have people
put them in envelopes,

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:08.170 align:middle line:84%
send them in the mail,
and then get them back

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:09.760 align:middle line:90%
and sort them again.

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:11.260 align:middle line:90%
Netflix also had just--

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:13.210 align:middle line:84%
so if you look at
Netflix's accounting data

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:15.730 align:middle line:84%
and you look at how much
they spend buying DVDs,

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:17.890 align:middle line:84%
how much do they spend
on their mail rooms,

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:20.740 align:middle line:84%
you can get very good
accounting estimates of,

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:24.620 align:middle line:84%
what is Netflix's cost
of having a subscriber?

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:28.250 align:middle line:84%
So he treats this as
this is a product where

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:31.940 align:middle line:84%
we can observe demand well, and
we know the cost from accounting

00:33:31.940 --> 00:33:34.640 align:middle line:90%
data.

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:40.130 align:middle line:84%
Big limitation, there
is no price variation.

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:42.500 align:middle line:84%
Netflix did have some
coupons or whatever,

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:44.057 align:middle line:90%
but he doesn't know that.

00:33:44.057 --> 00:33:45.890 align:middle line:84%
He doesn't know what
most of his subscribers

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:47.348 align:middle line:84%
paid for Netflix
because he doesn't

00:33:47.348 --> 00:33:48.643 align:middle line:90%
observe them buying Netflix.

00:33:48.643 --> 00:33:50.810 align:middle line:84%
He doesn't know what kind
of subscription they have,

00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:53.030 align:middle line:84%
whether it's a
two-disc subscription

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:55.680 align:middle line:84%
or a three-disc subscription
or something like that.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:34:00.800 align:middle line:84%
So normally you think of, I
need exogenous price variation

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:02.960 align:middle line:90%
to estimate demand elasticities.

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:07.730 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the
second clever insight

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.770 align:middle line:84%
that he makes in the
paper, besides that Netflix

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:11.540 align:middle line:84%
is a good experimental
subject, is

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:16.310 align:middle line:84%
that you can try to estimate
price elasticities here

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:18.179 align:middle line:90%
without any price variation.

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:22.370 align:middle line:84%
So Tobias is going to talk
about demand estimation

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:24.500 align:middle line:84%
in a couple of weeks,
which can't come too soon,

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:25.917 align:middle line:84%
it seems, the
papers I'm covering.

00:34:25.917 --> 00:34:28.133 align:middle line:90%


00:34:28.133 --> 00:34:29.550 align:middle line:84%
A basic insight
of that literature

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:31.440 align:middle line:84%
is, if you have exogenous
price variation,

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:33.840 align:middle line:84%
you can estimate
demand elasticities.

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:37.350 align:middle line:84%
And then once you've estimated
the demand elasticities,

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:40.469 align:middle line:84%
if you're willing to assume that
firms are profit maximizing,

00:34:40.469 --> 00:34:44.580 align:middle line:84%
then you've got this condition
that the firm is maximizing

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:50.070 align:middle line:84%
over p, p minus c
D of p, or the firm

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:52.469 align:middle line:84%
is going to be doing
p minus c over p

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.250 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 over the
elasticity of demand.

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:57.270 align:middle line:84%
So if I observe the
elasticity of demand

00:34:57.270 --> 00:35:00.000 align:middle line:84%
and I observe the
prices, I can figure out

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.460 align:middle line:84%
what the firm's costs
are without any cost data

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:08.220 align:middle line:84%
if I assume that firms are
profit maximizing monopolists.

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:11.970 align:middle line:84%
What Shiller notes is that if
you think about this equation,

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:15.060 align:middle line:84%
let's suppose that we
know that costs are known

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:18.240 align:middle line:84%
and Netflix is
profit maximizing.

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:22.050 align:middle line:84%
If costs are known and
Netflix is profit maximizing,

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:25.530 align:middle line:84%
then I know p and
I know c and I know

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:29.800 align:middle line:84%
p, which means I can
infer what the epsilon is.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:35.350 align:middle line:84%
So having the knowledge of
what fraction of people buy

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:40.540 align:middle line:84%
lets me get dq / dp
because epsilon is dq / dp

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:42.110 align:middle line:90%
times p over q or whatever.

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:46.480 align:middle line:84%
So if I know p and I know q,
then I can also get dq / dp.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:48.855 align:middle line:90%
So that gives me dq / dp.

00:35:48.855 --> 00:35:53.300 align:middle line:90%


00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:57.500 align:middle line:84%
So I can really do
what we often want

00:35:57.500 --> 00:36:01.580 align:middle line:84%
to get out of demand estimation,
even without any exogenous price

00:36:01.580 --> 00:36:04.100 align:middle line:84%
variation, if I have
cost variation and profit

00:36:04.100 --> 00:36:05.990 align:middle line:90%
maximization.

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:08.790 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, what does he do?

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:12.590 align:middle line:84%
So again, it looks like a lot of
the logit-like demand structures

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:13.850 align:middle line:90%
I talked about earlier.

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:15.920 align:middle line:84%
He assumes that
consumer i's utility

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:24.170 align:middle line:84%
from buying Netflix package j
is some utility from Netflix

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:28.620 align:middle line:84%
minus alpha times the
price of the package.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:30.980 align:middle line:84%
So alpha is this
price sensitivity.

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:35.870 align:middle line:84%
Plus xi j, which is the
quality of package j,

00:36:35.870 --> 00:36:38.120 align:middle line:84%
plus this random
error term that makes

00:36:38.120 --> 00:36:43.740 align:middle line:84%
some people who are similar
buy and some people not buy.

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:46.830 align:middle line:84%
And he assumes that
this average willingness

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:51.930 align:middle line:84%
to pay for Netflix among
people with demographics X

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:54.110 align:middle line:90%
is Xi times beta.

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:57.120 align:middle line:84%
And he's going to think about
that Netflix could discriminate

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:58.710 align:middle line:84%
by charging different
prices to people

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:01.020 align:middle line:90%
with different observables Xi.

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:04.720 align:middle line:84%
And in his data set, there
are all kinds of observables.

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:08.550 align:middle line:84%
So he has 18
demographic variables,

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:13.170 align:middle line:84%
15 variables that relate to the
quantity and timing of when you

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:18.930 align:middle line:84%
are on the internet, but then he
puts in 4,600 other variables,

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:21.480 align:middle line:84%
recording things like,
how much time do you spend

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:23.528 align:middle line:90%
on microsoftnetwork.com?

00:37:23.528 --> 00:37:25.570 align:middle line:84%
How much time do you spend
on the New York Times?

00:37:25.570 --> 00:37:27.370 align:middle line:84%
How much time do you
spend on Gamefly?

00:37:27.370 --> 00:37:30.340 align:middle line:84%
How much time do you spend
on many other websites?

00:37:30.340 --> 00:37:34.860 align:middle line:84%
And so he basically takes the
time spent on the 4,600 most

00:37:34.860 --> 00:37:40.290 align:middle line:84%
popular websites as a 4,600
dimensional description of you.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:48.050 align:middle line:84%
And then, in his model,
there's only a single alpha.

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:51.310 align:middle line:90%
So dq / dp is the same for--

00:37:51.310 --> 00:37:53.020 align:middle line:84%
well, not dq / dp,
but d (utility)

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:55.990 align:middle line:84%
/ d(p) is the same
for everybody.

00:37:55.990 --> 00:38:04.247 align:middle line:84%
But because the mean
utilities differ,

00:38:04.247 --> 00:38:06.580 align:middle line:84%
people who have a very high
v are going to buy the thing

00:38:06.580 --> 00:38:08.630 align:middle line:84%
basically almost regardless
of what the price is.

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:11.720 align:middle line:84%
People with a very low v are
highly unlikely to buy it.

00:38:11.720 --> 00:38:14.650 align:middle line:84%
And so the price sensitivity
does differ across people

00:38:14.650 --> 00:38:17.770 align:middle line:84%
because you have these
nonlinearities in how much

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:19.270 align:middle line:84%
it's worth to you,
and that affects

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:21.170 align:middle line:90%
how much you're willing to pay.

00:38:21.170 --> 00:38:29.430 align:middle line:84%
And so then he's going to
estimate these beta parameters.

00:38:29.430 --> 00:38:31.180 align:middle line:84%
He's going to estimate
the beta parameters

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:36.050 align:middle line:84%
via a Lasso-like maximum
likelihood estimation.

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:38.480 align:middle line:84%
Do people here know
what Lasso models are?

00:38:38.480 --> 00:38:38.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:38:38.980 --> 00:38:45.880 align:middle line:84%
So Lasso model, you're used
to seeing simple regression.

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:47.630 align:middle line:90%
How does OLS regression work?

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:53.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm just trying to maximize the
sum of y minus yi minus beta Xi

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:55.030 align:middle line:90%
squared.

00:38:55.030 --> 00:38:58.120 align:middle line:84%
And this is my OLS
estimator of beta.

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:03.730 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, I minimize over beta
y minus x beta squared.

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:05.980 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:09.995 align:middle line:84%
The lasso model is,
suppose you have--

00:39:09.995 --> 00:39:12.370 align:middle line:84%
and something you're taught
in undergraduate econometrics

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:14.380 align:middle line:84%
is if I'm going
to do this, I need

00:39:14.380 --> 00:39:17.270 align:middle line:84%
to have many fewer x's
than I have observations.

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:20.290 align:middle line:90%
And so I need the--

00:39:20.290 --> 00:39:23.260 align:middle line:84%
if the number of variables is N
and the number of observations

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:25.400 align:middle line:84%
is T, I need N
small relative to T.

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:28.510 align:middle line:84%
I need T going to infinity
relative to the number

00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:33.130 align:middle line:84%
of right-hand side variables in
order to estimate this equation.

00:39:33.130 --> 00:39:37.480 align:middle line:84%
What econometricians or computer
scientists and econometricians

00:39:37.480 --> 00:39:39.397 align:middle line:84%
have noticed is that
you don't actually

00:39:39.397 --> 00:39:41.230 align:middle line:84%
need that to estimate
a regression if you're

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:44.982 align:middle line:84%
willing to make the assumption
that most of the betas are 0.

00:39:44.982 --> 00:39:46.690 align:middle line:84%
And if you're willing
to-- the assumption

00:39:46.690 --> 00:39:48.107 align:middle line:84%
is you have a
regression where you

00:39:48.107 --> 00:39:50.180 align:middle line:84%
have many, many
right-hand side variables.

00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:52.210 align:middle line:90%
Most of the betas are 0.

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:54.470 align:middle line:84%
The only thing is, you
don't know which ones are 0.

00:39:54.470 --> 00:40:00.250 align:middle line:84%
Then what you can do is
instead minimize over beta

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:03.760 align:middle line:90%
the sum across observations i.

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:09.010 align:middle line:90%
yi minus Xi beta squared.

00:40:09.010 --> 00:40:18.995 align:middle line:84%
And then subtract off the
sum over all j of lambda j.

00:40:18.995 --> 00:40:20.120 align:middle line:90%
I actually want to do this.

00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:25.700 align:middle line:84%
Minus lambda the sum over
j absolute value beta j.

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:29.390 align:middle line:84%
So I'm using i to index
people, j to index

00:40:29.390 --> 00:40:31.700 align:middle line:90%
variables in the equation.

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:34.640 align:middle line:84%
So if I instead
minimize an objective

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:36.320 align:middle line:84%
function like this,
where I'm minimizing

00:40:36.320 --> 00:40:39.380 align:middle line:84%
the sum of squared
residuals minus the sum

00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:42.020 align:middle line:84%
of the absolute values
of the coefficients.

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:45.020 align:middle line:84%
And because you're minimizing
the sum of the absolute value

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.580 align:middle line:84%
of the coefficients,
it's like you're

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:49.880 align:middle line:84%
putting a penalty function
that looks like this if you

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:52.230 align:middle line:90%
use a nonzero beta j.

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:55.940 align:middle line:84%
And so anything where
the beta j looks like it

00:40:55.940 --> 00:40:57.832 align:middle line:84%
has a very, very
small effect, you

00:40:57.832 --> 00:40:59.540 align:middle line:84%
minimize a function
like this with a kink

00:40:59.540 --> 00:41:02.810 align:middle line:84%
by just setting
beta j equal to 0.

00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:05.570 align:middle line:84%
And so what you do
in a Lasso model is

00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:07.460 align:middle line:84%
you estimate a
regression like this,

00:41:07.460 --> 00:41:10.070 align:middle line:84%
and you put in these
penalty functions.

00:41:10.070 --> 00:41:11.540 align:middle line:84%
And you put those
penalty functions

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:14.600 align:middle line:84%
in in a way that sets most of
the coefficients equal to zero.

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:18.135 align:middle line:84%
And that is asymptotically
a valid estimate

00:41:18.135 --> 00:41:19.760 align:middle line:84%
under some set of
assumptions about how

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:25.070 align:middle line:84%
fast the number of nonzero
coefficients increases with t.

00:41:25.070 --> 00:41:26.060 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:28.670 align:middle line:84%
So he's doing
something very much

00:41:28.670 --> 00:41:31.010 align:middle line:84%
like that in maximum
likelihood estimation,

00:41:31.010 --> 00:41:36.080 align:middle line:84%
where what he's doing
is he's maximizing

00:41:36.080 --> 00:41:41.510 align:middle line:84%
the sum of the likelihood
of yi given Xi.

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:45.800 align:middle line:84%
And then also subtracting lambda
times the sum of the absolute

00:41:45.800 --> 00:41:47.480 align:middle line:90%
value of the beta j's.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:49.760 align:middle line:84%
So he's doing a maximum
likelihood version

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:52.640 align:middle line:84%
of that, where you're maximizing
the sum of the log likelihoods

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:55.040 align:middle line:84%
and subtracting this penalty
function for every nonzero

00:41:55.040 --> 00:41:56.320 align:middle line:90%
coefficient.

00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:59.630 align:middle line:84%
And so what his model
does in practice, I mean,

00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:03.420 align:middle line:84%
I'm a little surprised at
how many variables he keeps,

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:06.570 align:middle line:84%
but roughly he keeps 900
of the right-hand side

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:08.230 align:middle line:90%
variables in the equation.

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:11.550 align:middle line:84%
But then it's as if the other
3,700 variables get dropped from

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:14.490 align:middle line:84%
the equation because the beta
estimates are so small that you

00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:15.455 align:middle line:90%
just set them to 0.

00:42:15.455 --> 00:42:19.240 align:middle line:90%


00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:22.300 align:middle line:84%
Just to give a sense of
what his data looks like,

00:42:22.300 --> 00:42:25.390 align:middle line:84%
the demographics are things
like the age of the people.

00:42:25.390 --> 00:42:29.140 align:middle line:84%
Is the oldest person in the
household, 21 to 24, 25 to 29,

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:30.940 align:middle line:90%
30 to 34?

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:32.450 align:middle line:90%
He's got race in there.

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:34.600 align:middle line:90%
He's got income in there.

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:37.570 align:middle line:84%
And then the browsing
habits, he's got things like,

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:40.810 align:middle line:84%
what fraction of your internet
use is midnight to 6:00 AM?

00:42:40.810 --> 00:42:43.100 align:middle line:84%
What fraction is
6:00 AM to 9:00 AM?

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:45.145 align:middle line:84%
What fraction is
6:00 PM to midnight?

00:42:45.145 --> 00:42:47.770 align:middle line:84%
How much do you use the internet
on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:49.490 align:middle line:90%
Thursday, Friday, Saturday?

00:42:49.490 --> 00:42:54.190 align:middle line:84%
So he has this set of
minimal demographics

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:57.200 align:middle line:84%
on people, although more
than you would often see.

00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:59.690 align:middle line:84%
At least he does have some
set of income brackets.

00:42:59.690 --> 00:43:04.030 align:middle line:84%
And then he's got all of
your 4,600 website visits.

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:07.862 align:middle line:84%
And the results come out that
the most powerful predictor

00:43:07.862 --> 00:43:09.820 align:middle line:84%
of whether you're going
to subscribe to Netflix

00:43:09.820 --> 00:43:13.210 align:middle line:84%
is whether you're a frequent
visitor to Gamefly.com.

00:43:13.210 --> 00:43:14.990 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
Gamefly still exists.

00:43:14.990 --> 00:43:17.110 align:middle line:84%
Gamefly was a
company like Netflix,

00:43:17.110 --> 00:43:21.340 align:middle line:84%
but that mailed you
video game CD-ROMs.

00:43:21.340 --> 00:43:24.940 align:middle line:84%
So you would just have a
CD-ROM subscription to Gamefly.

00:43:24.940 --> 00:43:26.693 align:middle line:90%
You're playing Call of Duty 3.

00:43:26.693 --> 00:43:28.610 align:middle line:84%
You get tired of it, you
mail it back to them,

00:43:28.610 --> 00:43:30.580 align:middle line:84%
and they send you the next
video game on your list.

00:43:30.580 --> 00:43:32.020 align:middle line:84%
You play that for a
month, you send it back,

00:43:32.020 --> 00:43:33.830 align:middle line:84%
they send you the
next one on your list.

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:34.330 align:middle line:90%
But anyway.

00:43:34.330 --> 00:43:38.020 align:middle line:84%
So subscription to gamefly.com
was the most powerful

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:38.920 align:middle line:90%
positive predictor.

00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:40.420 align:middle line:84%
The most powerful
negative predictor

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:46.540 align:middle line:84%
of having a Netflix subscription
was, I don't know, ameblo.jp.

00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:50.560 align:middle line:84%
Some kind of Japanese
blogging site.

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:52.220 align:middle line:84%
People, I guess,
who apparently visit

00:43:52.220 --> 00:43:55.190 align:middle line:84%
Japanese blogging sites
don't do a lot of watching

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:56.160 align:middle line:90%
Netflix movies.

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:58.070 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:43:58.070 --> 00:44:01.700 align:middle line:84%
People who visit
audible.com and rent music

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:03.675 align:middle line:90%
also tend to rent DVDs.

00:44:03.675 --> 00:44:06.560 align:middle line:90%


00:44:06.560 --> 00:44:10.550 align:middle line:84%
People who do things like
visit jacksonville.com,

00:44:10.550 --> 00:44:15.350 align:middle line:84%
they don't watch
a lot of Netflix.

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:17.000 align:middle line:90%
You get the sense it's sort of--

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:19.100 align:middle line:84%
obviously he has
the age in there,

00:44:19.100 --> 00:44:21.860 align:middle line:84%
but there are people
who sit around and read

00:44:21.860 --> 00:44:27.350 align:middle line:84%
local news on the internet and
people who watch entertainment.

00:44:27.350 --> 00:44:29.360 align:middle line:90%
And it is--

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:30.540 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:33.770 align:middle line:84%
I find this
interesting, that, yes,

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:36.353 align:middle line:84%
once you think about it, yes,
many, many, many of these things

00:44:36.353 --> 00:44:38.603 align:middle line:84%
make sense if I'm trying to
predict whether someone is

00:44:38.603 --> 00:44:39.890 align:middle line:90%
likely to buy Netflix or not.

00:44:39.890 --> 00:44:42.390 align:middle line:84%
There's a tremendous amount of
information in their website.

00:44:42.390 --> 00:44:43.920 align:middle line:84%
And people are very,
very different,

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:46.580 align:middle line:84%
and it could be revealed through
these many different channels

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:47.486 align:middle line:90%
simultaneously.

00:44:47.486 --> 00:44:51.600 align:middle line:90%


00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:52.100 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:53.750 align:middle line:90%
What does he find?

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:59.360 align:middle line:84%
First finding here is that
demographic targeting--

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:01.765 align:middle line:84%
and again, remember, he
had the same demographics

00:45:01.765 --> 00:45:03.390 align:middle line:84%
we were talking about
in the car paper.

00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:08.120 align:middle line:84%
He does have-- he has
race, he has Hispanic.

00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:10.080 align:middle line:90%
He's actually got income.

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:12.440 align:middle line:84%
All of those things,
he says, are basically

00:45:12.440 --> 00:45:14.750 align:middle line:90%
useless for pricing Netflix.

00:45:14.750 --> 00:45:17.870 align:middle line:90%
He said that if you estimate--

00:45:17.870 --> 00:45:19.910 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to estimate
demand elasticities

00:45:19.910 --> 00:45:21.950 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the
demographic group

00:45:21.950 --> 00:45:24.500 align:middle line:84%
and think about switching
from the optimal uniform price

00:45:24.500 --> 00:45:27.930 align:middle line:84%
to the optimal demographic
targeted price.

00:45:27.930 --> 00:45:30.560 align:middle line:84%
And he finds that
targeting the demographics

00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:33.060 align:middle line:84%
would let Netflix increase
its profits by 0.3%.

00:45:33.060 --> 00:45:35.690 align:middle line:90%


00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:37.310 align:middle line:84%
I think, though,
in some sense, this

00:45:37.310 --> 00:45:41.030 align:middle line:84%
is consistent with what
I said before in that,

00:45:41.030 --> 00:45:42.200 align:middle line:90%
again, with that sort of--

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:45.380 align:middle line:84%
if profits are quadratic
in the extent to which you

00:45:45.380 --> 00:45:47.150 align:middle line:90%
want to change the price--

00:45:47.150 --> 00:45:50.580 align:middle line:84%
and profit, it's a
second order change.

00:45:50.580 --> 00:45:53.710 align:middle line:84%
You only want to make changes
on the order of a few percent,

00:45:53.710 --> 00:45:56.130 align:middle line:84%
then the profit effect
from that has to be small.

00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:57.420 align:middle line:90%
And that's what he says.

00:45:57.420 --> 00:46:01.270 align:middle line:90%
The profit impact is 0.3%.

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:05.140 align:middle line:84%
He argues that targeting on
the web, though, in some sense,

00:46:05.140 --> 00:46:09.070 align:middle line:84%
may be much more common than
targeting in the offline world

00:46:09.070 --> 00:46:11.920 align:middle line:84%
would be because these browsing
variables are much more

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:12.950 align:middle line:90%
powerful.

00:46:12.950 --> 00:46:15.700 align:middle line:84%
He estimates that they would let
you increase Netflix's profits

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:18.000 align:middle line:90%
by 13%.

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:20.500 align:middle line:84%
It's still not a huge number
when you think about you've got

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:22.670 align:middle line:90%
those 4,600 variables in there.

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:24.508 align:middle line:84%
What could you do
with all of those?

00:46:24.508 --> 00:46:26.050 align:middle line:84%
He says it's much,
much more than you

00:46:26.050 --> 00:46:28.870 align:middle line:84%
can do with demographics,
but it's 13%.

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:33.360 align:middle line:90%


00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:36.200 align:middle line:90%
What are the welfare effects?

00:46:36.200 --> 00:46:37.230 align:middle line:90%
Oh, do I have it?

00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:37.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:37.980 --> 00:46:42.540 align:middle line:90%
So welfare effects are--

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:46.050 align:middle line:84%
here he's arguing that
with demographic targeting,

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:47.970 align:middle line:90%
we're actually--

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:52.620 align:middle line:84%
in this diagram, it's the
tiniest arrow in the world,

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:54.370 align:middle line:90%
but it's an arrow to the right.

00:46:54.370 --> 00:46:58.360 align:middle line:84%
That is, the profits
are going up by 0.3%.

00:46:58.360 --> 00:47:04.230 align:middle line:84%
The consumer surplus
is going up by 0.05%.

00:47:04.230 --> 00:47:08.230 align:middle line:84%
Demographic targeting, it's
like this kind of arrow.

00:47:08.230 --> 00:47:11.310 align:middle line:84%
So it's a bigger
arrow, going up 13%.

00:47:11.310 --> 00:47:17.700 align:middle line:84%
And it's going slightly down
on the consumer surplus side.

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:19.770 align:middle line:84%
Total social welfare,
I assume, has

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:22.560 align:middle line:84%
got to be going
up because you've

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:24.450 align:middle line:90%
got the 13% increase in profit.

00:47:24.450 --> 00:47:26.920 align:middle line:84%
The only negative, a half
percent on consumer surplus.

00:47:26.920 --> 00:47:28.890 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what the
mean consumer surplus is

00:47:28.890 --> 00:47:33.610 align:middle line:84%
versus profits, but assuming
that they're roughly comparable,

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:36.713 align:middle line:84%
social welfare has got to
be going up in both cases.

00:47:36.713 --> 00:47:39.650 align:middle line:90%


00:47:39.650 --> 00:47:43.250 align:middle line:84%
He does not have nearly
as much data to do this.

00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:46.390 align:middle line:84%
But he says, if Netflix were to
use second degree discrimination

00:47:46.390 --> 00:47:52.300 align:middle line:84%
instead, discriminating across
people by offering the one CD

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:55.970 align:middle line:84%
plan, the two DVD plan,
and the three DVD plan,

00:47:55.970 --> 00:47:58.090 align:middle line:84%
there, he argues that
second degree discrimination

00:47:58.090 --> 00:48:00.210 align:middle line:90%
would be much more powerful.

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:02.468 align:middle line:84%
And he claims, based on a
conversation with a Netflix

00:48:02.468 --> 00:48:04.510 align:middle line:84%
executive, that they were
not doing second degree

00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:06.350 align:middle line:90%
discrimination at the time.

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:09.130 align:middle line:90%
He said that they were doing--

00:48:09.130 --> 00:48:12.580 align:middle line:84%
plans are priced proportional to
cost with a single multiplier,

00:48:12.580 --> 00:48:14.200 align:middle line:84%
whereas secondary
discrimination model

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:16.930 align:middle line:84%
says you should be
charging different markups

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:19.390 align:middle line:90%
to different groups.

00:48:19.390 --> 00:48:20.050 align:middle line:90%
But anyway.

00:48:20.050 --> 00:48:22.180 align:middle line:84%
He says that secondary
discrimination could raise

00:48:22.180 --> 00:48:24.970 align:middle line:84%
profits by much more and
is a much bigger deal

00:48:24.970 --> 00:48:29.560 align:middle line:84%
than the third degree
discrimination they can do.

00:48:29.560 --> 00:48:33.580 align:middle line:84%
And here's an example
of what he's estimating,

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:35.860 align:middle line:90%
the consumer types.

00:48:35.860 --> 00:48:39.173 align:middle line:84%
If you have no
demographics at all,

00:48:39.173 --> 00:48:40.840 align:middle line:84%
it's as if you're
assuming that everyone

00:48:40.840 --> 00:48:43.750 align:middle line:84%
has the constant
mean willingness

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:45.860 align:middle line:90%
to pay v for the product.

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:47.620 align:middle line:84%
If you put in the
demographics, it's

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:51.520 align:middle line:84%
basically saying, instead of
having a spike here, almost

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:54.280 align:middle line:84%
everybody has one of
these two willingness

00:48:54.280 --> 00:48:56.350 align:middle line:84%
to pay that are very
close to each other.

00:48:56.350 --> 00:48:59.320 align:middle line:84%
And then when you look at the
full model with web browsing

00:48:59.320 --> 00:49:04.690 align:middle line:84%
data, you get this bigger
distribution like that.

00:49:04.690 --> 00:49:07.690 align:middle line:84%
And if you remember back to what
I said about Bergemann, Brooks,

00:49:07.690 --> 00:49:10.880 align:middle line:84%
Morris, it's the shape of
this distribution that's

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:13.190 align:middle line:84%
going to tend to determine
whether consumer surplus is

00:49:13.190 --> 00:49:14.640 align:middle line:90%
going up or down.

00:49:14.640 --> 00:49:17.810 align:middle line:84%
And it seems like the
shape of this distribution

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:20.330 align:middle line:84%
is something to the fact that
it keeps them from raising

00:49:20.330 --> 00:49:21.990 align:middle line:90%
their prices by too much.

00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:25.370 align:middle line:84%
And that's why that
would have to be

00:49:25.370 --> 00:49:30.200 align:middle line:84%
involved in consumer
surplus not going down a lot

00:49:30.200 --> 00:49:30.910 align:middle line:90%
when you do this.

00:49:30.910 --> 00:49:36.350 align:middle line:90%


00:49:36.350 --> 00:49:38.070 align:middle line:90%
Questions on Shiller?

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:38.632 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:49:38.632 --> 00:49:39.840 align:middle line:90%
I think I had one more slide.

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:41.210 align:middle line:90%
So some comments.

00:49:41.210 --> 00:49:44.360 align:middle line:90%


00:49:44.360 --> 00:49:47.330 align:middle line:84%
Again, I think this is
a nice, creative paper

00:49:47.330 --> 00:49:50.470 align:middle line:84%
if you think about what
it takes to write papers

00:49:50.470 --> 00:49:51.640 align:middle line:90%
as graduate students.

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:53.320 align:middle line:90%
He had this sort of--

00:49:53.320 --> 00:49:55.330 align:middle line:84%
just uses an
off-the-shelf data set,

00:49:55.330 --> 00:49:57.730 align:middle line:84%
and he has these
two different ideas.

00:49:57.730 --> 00:50:00.550 align:middle line:84%
One is this identification
idea that, if I

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:02.770 align:middle line:84%
can find a product where
I think cost is known,

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:04.360 align:middle line:84%
I can estimate
demand elasticities

00:50:04.360 --> 00:50:07.430 align:middle line:84%
without having any price
variation in the data.

00:50:07.430 --> 00:50:11.500 align:middle line:84%
And Netflix is a product
that's both very popular

00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:14.200 align:middle line:84%
and where I can track
usage and where I can

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:16.215 align:middle line:90%
get the observable cost data.

00:50:16.215 --> 00:50:19.440 align:middle line:90%


00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:21.210 align:middle line:84%
He also has this
other nice observation

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:24.360 align:middle line:84%
where I can do this comparison
of demographic price

00:50:24.360 --> 00:50:29.430 align:middle line:84%
discrimination versus web
browsing price discrimination.

00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:32.100 align:middle line:84%
I think these are important
applied observations

00:50:32.100 --> 00:50:36.300 align:middle line:84%
to the extent that, obviously,
the limitations of the data

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:38.640 align:middle line:90%
may make it--

00:50:38.640 --> 00:50:41.820 align:middle line:84%
it's a good data point, but I
don't know how much we believe.

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:44.610 align:middle line:84%
But to the extent we
believe that, it suggests

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:47.070 align:middle line:84%
that demographics are of
very little value for price

00:50:47.070 --> 00:50:48.450 align:middle line:90%
discrimination.

00:50:48.450 --> 00:50:49.980 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we really
shouldn't expect

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:52.800 align:middle line:84%
to see much demographic
price discrimination.

00:50:52.800 --> 00:50:56.980 align:middle line:84%
It shows that web browsing can
reveal a lot of information.

00:50:56.980 --> 00:51:00.390 align:middle line:84%
And something else I would say
is that it also shows that web

00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:02.820 align:middle line:84%
browsing-based
discrimination is--

00:51:02.820 --> 00:51:04.560 align:middle line:84%
one obstacle to
discrimination is always

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:07.260 align:middle line:90%
people hiding who they are.

00:51:07.260 --> 00:51:08.850 align:middle line:90%
If you try to have a student--

00:51:08.850 --> 00:51:11.613 align:middle line:84%
imagine you try to have a
student surcharge at ART.

00:51:11.613 --> 00:51:13.530 align:middle line:84%
If you go there and
you're a graduate student,

00:51:13.530 --> 00:51:17.370 align:middle line:84%
they charge you 20% more than
someone who's not a student.

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:19.510 align:middle line:84%
It would be very
hard to get someone

00:51:19.510 --> 00:51:21.933 align:middle line:84%
to prove they're not
a student to you.

00:51:21.933 --> 00:51:23.350 align:middle line:84%
That price
discrimination wouldn't

00:51:23.350 --> 00:51:25.750 align:middle line:84%
work because it's hard to
prove that you're not actually

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:28.540 align:middle line:90%
in school somewhere.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:34.120 align:middle line:84%
Here, it would be very hard
to distort your web browsing

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:37.240 align:middle line:84%
behavior in a way that prevented
Netflix from discriminating

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:40.990 align:middle line:84%
against you because Netflix
can discriminate against you

00:51:40.990 --> 00:51:45.350 align:middle line:84%
by looking at your visits
to 4,000 different websites.

00:51:45.350 --> 00:51:49.120 align:middle line:84%
And OK, maybe I'm going to stay
off gamefly.com in the month

00:51:49.120 --> 00:51:51.620 align:middle line:84%
before I'm trying to buy Netflix
to get my price to go down.

00:51:51.620 --> 00:51:54.162 align:middle line:84%
But that's just-- they're only
putting three times the weight

00:51:54.162 --> 00:51:59.460 align:middle line:84%
on that as they use on these
thousands of other websites.

00:51:59.460 --> 00:52:02.250 align:middle line:84%
And so you really would have
to do a tremendous amount

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:04.930 align:middle line:84%
of distorting your behavior to
try to save money on Netflix.

00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:07.650 align:middle line:84%
And given that it's distorting
your behavior on 4,000 other

00:52:07.650 --> 00:52:09.870 align:middle line:84%
websites to get whatever
discount they're giving you,

00:52:09.870 --> 00:52:13.590 align:middle line:84%
it's just going to be impossible
for consumers to hide who they

00:52:13.590 --> 00:52:17.340 align:middle line:84%
are, other than consumers
hiding who they are via some

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:21.387 align:middle line:84%
technological means, like some
tracking blocker software.

00:52:21.387 --> 00:52:23.220 align:middle line:84%
But then again, obviously
what we don't know

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:25.170 align:middle line:84%
is, what's the mean willingness
to pay in people who

00:52:25.170 --> 00:52:26.483 align:middle line:90%
use tracker blocking software?

00:52:26.483 --> 00:52:27.650 align:middle line:90%
Do they pay even more money?

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:31.080 align:middle line:90%


00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:34.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Was Netflix able to
see people's browser activity

00:52:34.020 --> 00:52:36.000 align:middle line:90%
back during this time?

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:40.460 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: So yeah.

00:52:40.460 --> 00:52:44.120 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, there are many
data brokers out there

00:52:44.120 --> 00:52:48.710 align:middle line:84%
who stick cookies
on your computer

00:52:48.710 --> 00:52:50.190 align:middle line:90%
when you visit websites.

00:52:50.190 --> 00:52:53.300 align:middle line:84%
And Netflix could
have made agreements

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:55.670 align:middle line:84%
with some of these
firms to buy their data

00:52:55.670 --> 00:52:57.730 align:middle line:84%
and get the tracking
data from you.

00:52:57.730 --> 00:53:00.470 align:middle line:90%


00:53:00.470 --> 00:53:04.310 align:middle line:84%
I think in 2006, we
know Netflix was--

00:53:04.310 --> 00:53:07.760 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they're just basically
doing $6.99 if you want one DVD,

00:53:07.760 --> 00:53:08.420 align:middle line:90%
$8.99.

00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:12.140 align:middle line:84%
They weren't doing any
discrimination whatsoever.

00:53:12.140 --> 00:53:16.010 align:middle line:84%
I don't know when those data
markets developed, but it

00:53:16.010 --> 00:53:17.720 align:middle line:84%
certainly wasn't long
after this that you

00:53:17.720 --> 00:53:22.363 align:middle line:84%
could purchase an awful lot of
data about users from many--

00:53:22.363 --> 00:53:23.780 align:middle line:84%
there are many
businesses who have

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:25.880 align:middle line:84%
this model of sticking
cookies on your machine

00:53:25.880 --> 00:53:27.965 align:middle line:84%
and then selling the
data about you every time

00:53:27.965 --> 00:53:28.840 align:middle line:90%
you visit their site.

00:53:28.840 --> 00:53:29.465 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:53:29.465 --> 00:53:34.860 align:middle line:90%


00:53:34.860 --> 00:53:37.745 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Obviously,
critical aspects of the paper.

00:53:37.745 --> 00:53:41.368 align:middle line:90%


00:53:41.368 --> 00:53:43.410 align:middle line:84%
It's a very nice observation
that you can do this

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:46.440 align:middle line:84%
without any price variation, but
it takes some strong assumptions

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:48.270 align:middle line:90%
to do this.

00:53:48.270 --> 00:53:50.310 align:middle line:90%
I really would like to have--

00:53:50.310 --> 00:54:00.930 align:middle line:84%
he's got-- he had vi minus alpha
pj plus xi j plus epsilon ij.

00:54:00.930 --> 00:54:03.810 align:middle line:90%
He's got the vi is Xi beta.

00:54:03.810 --> 00:54:06.030 align:middle line:84%
You would really like
to have the alpha also

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:08.940 align:middle line:90%
be Xi times gamma.

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:11.880 align:middle line:84%
You would really like to be able
to get at variation in the alpha

00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:13.505 align:middle line:84%
separate from the
variation in the v's.

00:54:13.505 --> 00:54:17.954 align:middle line:84%
And maybe we would also
like a coefficient here too.

00:54:17.954 --> 00:54:20.940 align:middle line:90%
Xi times eta times the xi j.

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:23.520 align:middle line:84%
Like do people-- also,
does their valuation

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:25.170 align:middle line:84%
for high quality
versus low quality

00:54:25.170 --> 00:54:26.920 align:middle line:84%
also differ with
their demographics?

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:32.560 align:middle line:84%
So you've just got limited
things that you can do.

00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:34.740 align:middle line:84%
And another thing
we have is that,

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:36.740 align:middle line:84%
what are the properties
of the utility function?

00:54:36.740 --> 00:54:39.490 align:middle line:84%
If you remember, I said,
with linear demand curves,

00:54:39.490 --> 00:54:41.410 align:middle line:84%
we know that price
discrimination is always

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:44.020 align:middle line:90%
bad for welfare.

00:54:44.020 --> 00:54:49.060 align:middle line:84%
And so does this
model with variation

00:54:49.060 --> 00:54:51.670 align:middle line:84%
in v's, does it
have properties that

00:54:51.670 --> 00:54:54.722 align:middle line:84%
make it so that the consumer
surplus doesn't go down much?

00:54:54.722 --> 00:54:56.680 align:middle line:84%
Or is that something that
he's estimated, or is

00:54:56.680 --> 00:54:59.177 align:middle line:90%
that something he's assumed?

00:54:59.177 --> 00:55:01.510 align:middle line:84%
I don't know so much about
the properties of this demand

00:55:01.510 --> 00:55:03.010 align:middle line:84%
system, but in some
sense you'd like

00:55:03.010 --> 00:55:05.830 align:middle line:84%
to try to get at, what are the
properties of the demand system

00:55:05.830 --> 00:55:08.770 align:middle line:90%
that can do that or not?

00:55:08.770 --> 00:55:10.597 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:10.597 --> 00:55:11.555 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:55:11.555 --> 00:55:14.420 align:middle line:90%


00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:14.920 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:14.920 --> 00:55:20.410 align:middle line:84%
So then the final paper I was
going to talk about today, Dubé

00:55:20.410 --> 00:55:24.730 align:middle line:90%
and Misra, it's an example.

00:55:24.730 --> 00:55:28.750 align:middle line:84%
This is sort of very
high quality data set,

00:55:28.750 --> 00:55:31.540 align:middle line:90%
very high quality econometrics.

00:55:31.540 --> 00:55:35.680 align:middle line:84%
Again, the paper you would love
to have as a job market paper.

00:55:35.680 --> 00:55:38.800 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, a big difficulty
of this job market paper--

00:55:38.800 --> 00:55:41.710 align:middle line:84%
this kind of paper
is it shows there

00:55:41.710 --> 00:55:45.190 align:middle line:84%
is this huge benefit
towards data in this world

00:55:45.190 --> 00:55:47.680 align:middle line:84%
and having
connections with firms

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:49.930 align:middle line:84%
that can generate
such data sets for you

00:55:49.930 --> 00:55:53.410 align:middle line:84%
and let you be the person who
can analyze such data sets.

00:55:53.410 --> 00:55:57.730 align:middle line:84%
So the particular
application is--

00:55:57.730 --> 00:56:00.020 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
people have heard of--

00:56:00.020 --> 00:56:01.700 align:middle line:84%
we are back in the
modern world now.

00:56:01.700 --> 00:56:03.940 align:middle line:90%
So this is ZipRecruiter.

00:56:03.940 --> 00:56:07.720 align:middle line:84%
ZipRecruiter is an
employment website.

00:56:07.720 --> 00:56:09.550 align:middle line:84%
If you're a small
firm or a big firm

00:56:09.550 --> 00:56:11.800 align:middle line:84%
and you want to
hire employees, you

00:56:11.800 --> 00:56:14.920 align:middle line:84%
can sign up for an
account with ZipRecruiter,

00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:17.690 align:middle line:84%
and then ask
ZipRecruiter to send you

00:56:17.690 --> 00:56:20.300 align:middle line:84%
resumes of people
who have signed up

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:24.000 align:middle line:84%
on ZipRecruiter saying, I would
like to be a software engineer.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:25.350 align:middle line:90%
I have these qualifications.

00:56:25.350 --> 00:56:28.070 align:middle line:84%
You can have ZipRecruiter
send you resumes

00:56:28.070 --> 00:56:32.420 align:middle line:84%
of people who are currently
looking for jobs, who meet

00:56:32.420 --> 00:56:36.170 align:middle line:90%
the description for your job.

00:56:36.170 --> 00:56:39.650 align:middle line:84%
The way ZipRecruiter
works is it's

00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:42.068 align:middle line:90%
free to use for job searchers.

00:56:42.068 --> 00:56:44.360 align:middle line:84%
If you're a job searcher,
you can just post your resume

00:56:44.360 --> 00:56:48.500 align:middle line:90%
on ZipRecruiter.com for free.

00:56:48.500 --> 00:56:51.320 align:middle line:84%
But then the firms who
are looking to hire people

00:56:51.320 --> 00:56:54.980 align:middle line:84%
have to pay a monthly fee
to be on ZipRecruiter.

00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:59.340 align:middle line:84%
The paper is dealing
with small employers.

00:56:59.340 --> 00:57:02.690 align:middle line:84%
The small firms are firms that
had 50 or fewer employees,

00:57:02.690 --> 00:57:05.330 align:middle line:84%
reported they were going to
search for fewer than one

00:57:05.330 --> 00:57:08.120 align:middle line:84%
to three employees
in the near future.

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:10.700 align:middle line:84%
And what ZipRecruiter would
do with such new firms

00:57:10.700 --> 00:57:15.210 align:middle line:84%
when they sign up is charge them
a $99 a month subscription fee.

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:16.980 align:middle line:84%
And so you can sign
up to ZipRecruiter.

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:18.170 align:middle line:90%
You pay $99 a month.

00:57:18.170 --> 00:57:21.575 align:middle line:84%
And then you post the
job that you want to get,

00:57:21.575 --> 00:57:23.700 align:middle line:84%
and they send you a bunch
of resumes that match it.

00:57:23.700 --> 00:57:26.000 align:middle line:84%
And then you can try to
interview or whatever,

00:57:26.000 --> 00:57:29.100 align:middle line:90%
look over those resumes.

00:57:29.100 --> 00:57:32.940 align:middle line:84%
This paper, they had internal
access to ZipRecruiter.

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:36.180 align:middle line:84%
And actually, ZipRecruiter
was willing to run

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:37.630 align:middle line:90%
experiments for them.

00:57:37.630 --> 00:57:42.930 align:middle line:84%
You get the sense that this is,
in some sense, a joint research

00:57:42.930 --> 00:57:45.990 align:middle line:84%
consulting kind of
relationship with ZipRecruiter,

00:57:45.990 --> 00:57:50.070 align:middle line:84%
where they get ZipRecruiter
to let them run experiments

00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:52.892 align:middle line:84%
on ZipRecruiter's pricing, and
then they report the results

00:57:52.892 --> 00:57:54.600 align:middle line:84%
back to ZipRecruiter
because ZipRecruiter

00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:57.540 align:middle line:84%
was interested in the question
of, what price should we

00:57:57.540 --> 00:57:58.040 align:middle line:90%
be charging?

00:57:58.040 --> 00:57:59.748 align:middle line:84%
Should we be discriminating
against firms

00:57:59.748 --> 00:58:02.350 align:middle line:84%
in charging some firms more
than we're charging other firms?

00:58:02.350 --> 00:58:06.300 align:middle line:84%
And can we make
more money that way?

00:58:06.300 --> 00:58:11.850 align:middle line:84%
So the way it worked is they
had this experiment where

00:58:11.850 --> 00:58:14.640 align:middle line:84%
if you go to ZipRecruiter
on the firm side,

00:58:14.640 --> 00:58:16.150 align:middle line:90%
you can't just sign up.

00:58:16.150 --> 00:58:20.310 align:middle line:84%
You have to enter in
information about your firm.

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:24.420 align:middle line:84%
So what are the things about
the firm you have to enter?

00:58:24.420 --> 00:58:27.600 align:middle line:84%
One-- oh, I guess not here--
is the number of employees that

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:29.003 align:middle line:90%
you-- this sort of basic--

00:58:29.003 --> 00:58:30.420 align:middle line:84%
to get into the
bucket at all, you

00:58:30.420 --> 00:58:32.810 align:middle line:84%
had to be the under 50
employees and looking

00:58:32.810 --> 00:58:35.100 align:middle line:90%
to hire one to three people.

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:37.170 align:middle line:84%
And then you have lots
of features of the jobs,

00:58:37.170 --> 00:58:39.160 align:middle line:84%
like where is the job
going to be located?

00:58:39.160 --> 00:58:40.960 align:middle line:90%
What type of company are you?

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:42.760 align:middle line:84%
How many people do
you need to hire?

00:58:42.760 --> 00:58:44.642 align:middle line:90%
Does the job provide benefits?

00:58:44.642 --> 00:58:45.850 align:middle line:90%
Do you need to have a resume?

00:58:45.850 --> 00:58:49.320 align:middle line:84%
Do you provide mental
benefits, dental benefits?

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:51.670 align:middle line:84%
What category does
the job fall in?

00:58:51.670 --> 00:58:53.610 align:middle line:84%
Is it an administrative
job, a technical job,

00:58:53.610 --> 00:58:57.150 align:middle line:90%
a programming job, whatever?

00:58:57.150 --> 00:59:00.220 align:middle line:84%
It's fairly minimal
evidence from a menu.

00:59:00.220 --> 00:59:03.060 align:middle line:84%
But you've got to do all
this before ZipRecruiter will

00:59:03.060 --> 00:59:04.600 align:middle line:90%
tell you what the price is.

00:59:04.600 --> 00:59:07.540 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, if you told
ZipRecruiter I'm a 50,000-person

00:59:07.540 --> 00:59:09.822 align:middle line:84%
firm, I'm going to hire
hundreds of people a month,

00:59:09.822 --> 00:59:12.280 align:middle line:84%
they were already discriminating
based on that information.

00:59:12.280 --> 00:59:13.988 align:middle line:84%
And they would say,
if you want to hire--

00:59:13.988 --> 00:59:16.330 align:middle line:84%
if you're a
10,000-person-- you're MIT,

00:59:16.330 --> 00:59:19.180 align:middle line:84%
you've got 15,000 employees,
you're going to use ZipRecruiter

00:59:19.180 --> 00:59:22.420 align:middle line:84%
to hire 1,000 people a year,
you'll get charged many,

00:59:22.420 --> 00:59:26.890 align:middle line:84%
many thousands of dollars
a month, not $99 a month.

00:59:26.890 --> 00:59:29.620 align:middle line:84%
But what they did in
the experiment was,

00:59:29.620 --> 00:59:32.020 align:middle line:84%
they had a month-long
experiment where

00:59:32.020 --> 00:59:34.540 align:middle line:84%
when you signed up, if you
answered everything that put you

00:59:34.540 --> 00:59:37.390 align:middle line:84%
in the low category, the
under 50 employee category,

00:59:37.390 --> 00:59:40.010 align:middle line:84%
they randomized what they
told you the monthly price was

00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:40.510 align:middle line:90%
going to be.

00:59:40.510 --> 00:59:41.920 align:middle line:90%
And they did it--

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:44.570 align:middle line:84%
$99 was the price they
were currently charging.

00:59:44.570 --> 00:59:46.570 align:middle line:84%
But then, in the
randomized sample,

00:59:46.570 --> 00:59:49.480 align:middle line:84%
some people were charged
$19, $39, $59, $79,

00:59:49.480 --> 00:59:52.330 align:middle line:84%
going all the way
up to $399 a month.

00:59:52.330 --> 00:59:55.900 align:middle line:84%
So they have this explicitly
randomized experiment

00:59:55.900 --> 00:59:58.360 align:middle line:84%
and an explicitly
randomized experiment

00:59:58.360 --> 01:00:00.670 align:middle line:84%
where they can think
about discriminating

01:00:00.670 --> 01:00:04.570 align:middle line:84%
based on the 133 features
they get out of this.

01:00:04.570 --> 01:00:06.580 align:middle line:90%
What are the 133 features?

01:00:06.580 --> 01:00:09.040 align:middle line:84%
You get a whole bunch of
them out of state dummies.

01:00:09.040 --> 01:00:12.430 align:middle line:84%
So you get 50 state dummies and
some Canadian province dummies.

01:00:12.430 --> 01:00:14.330 align:middle line:90%
You do get job categories.

01:00:14.330 --> 01:00:15.935 align:middle line:90%
You get benefits, yes or no.

01:00:15.935 --> 01:00:17.560 align:middle line:84%
Some of these are
binary, some are not.

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:19.607 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, they had 133,
quote, "features"

01:00:19.607 --> 01:00:21.690 align:middle line:84%
they could then think about
discriminating versus.

01:00:21.690 --> 01:00:26.080 align:middle line:90%


01:00:26.080 --> 01:00:29.000 align:middle line:84%
So the experiment,
it's a big experiment.

01:00:29.000 --> 01:00:31.450 align:middle line:84%
I think the experiment
was everyone who came

01:00:31.450 --> 01:00:34.870 align:middle line:84%
to ZipRecruiter that month, but
that's still a sample of just

01:00:34.870 --> 01:00:40.510 align:middle line:90%
7,867 firms.

01:00:40.510 --> 01:00:42.970 align:middle line:84%
Utility model is
very similar to what

01:00:42.970 --> 01:00:44.300 align:middle line:90%
you saw in the previous paper.

01:00:44.300 --> 01:00:46.690 align:middle line:84%
It's simpler here
because Netflix

01:00:46.690 --> 01:00:50.980 align:middle line:84%
had many different products that
needed xi j's to determine which

01:00:50.980 --> 01:00:53.480 align:middle line:90%
Netflix DVD level you bought.

01:00:53.480 --> 01:00:54.648 align:middle line:90%
Here, there's just one.

01:00:54.648 --> 01:00:56.440 align:middle line:84%
It's just like you go
through this process,

01:00:56.440 --> 01:00:58.040 align:middle line:90%
and it's either 1 or 0.

01:00:58.040 --> 01:01:01.870 align:middle line:84%
You click, yes, I'll give you my
credit card and sign up for $99

01:01:01.870 --> 01:01:04.930 align:middle line:84%
a month, or no, I don't
want to sign up now.

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:11.530 align:middle line:84%
So the utility is again, Xi
beta is your mean utility

01:01:11.530 --> 01:01:16.720 align:middle line:84%
given your characteristics, Xi,
for signing up for ZipRecruiter.

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:20.750 align:middle line:84%
They then have the alpha
coefficient vary across firms.

01:01:20.750 --> 01:01:24.350 align:middle line:84%
So you take the Xi multiplied
by another coefficient vector

01:01:24.350 --> 01:01:26.450 align:middle line:84%
gives you your
price sensitivity.

01:01:26.450 --> 01:01:29.090 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
an epsilon i again

01:01:29.090 --> 01:01:32.000 align:middle line:84%
logit error term at the
end, where it's just

01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:34.130 align:middle line:84%
some consumers with the
same characteristics

01:01:34.130 --> 01:01:37.970 align:middle line:84%
are willing to pay more or
less to be on ZipRecruiter.

01:01:37.970 --> 01:01:43.310 align:middle line:84%
And they estimate this by
three different procedures.

01:01:43.310 --> 01:01:47.870 align:middle line:84%
One is a pure maximum likelihood
estimation to estimate the--

01:01:47.870 --> 01:01:52.200 align:middle line:84%
again, this is 133 parameters
here, 133 parameters here.

01:01:52.200 --> 01:01:55.850 align:middle line:84%
So 266 parameters
to be estimated.

01:01:55.850 --> 01:01:58.680 align:middle line:90%
7,867 customers.

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:02.620 align:middle line:84%
One thing they do is just pure
maximum likelihood estimation.

01:02:02.620 --> 01:02:05.990 align:middle line:84%
Again, empirically,
people worry about over--

01:02:05.990 --> 01:02:11.030 align:middle line:84%
200 and whatever, 266, is too
many parameters to estimate with

01:02:11.030 --> 01:02:12.320 align:middle line:90%
7,000 observations.

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:14.410 align:middle line:90%
You end up overfitting the data.

01:02:14.410 --> 01:02:19.070 align:middle line:84%
So they use a Lasso-like
MLE procedure again

01:02:19.070 --> 01:02:24.480 align:middle line:84%
to estimate this, or maybe
it's a pure Lasso procedure.

01:02:24.480 --> 01:02:24.980 align:middle line:90%
I forget.

01:02:24.980 --> 01:02:28.040 align:middle line:84%
They use a Lasso procedure
also to estimate this,

01:02:28.040 --> 01:02:30.500 align:middle line:84%
setting more
coefficients equal to 0.

01:02:30.500 --> 01:02:35.120 align:middle line:84%
And then they also do a Weighted
Likelihood Bootstrap Lasso,

01:02:35.120 --> 01:02:36.540 align:middle line:90%
which you can look in the paper.

01:02:36.540 --> 01:02:38.490 align:middle line:84%
It takes up multiple
pages describing

01:02:38.490 --> 01:02:40.320 align:middle line:90%
what this procedure is.

01:02:40.320 --> 01:02:43.800 align:middle line:84%
The Weighted Likelihood
Bootstrap procedure,

01:02:43.800 --> 01:02:47.490 align:middle line:84%
the Lasso model essentially
sets some number of coefficients

01:02:47.490 --> 01:02:48.790 align:middle line:90%
equal to 0.

01:02:48.790 --> 01:02:50.310 align:middle line:84%
And all the inference
on it is based

01:02:50.310 --> 01:02:51.555 align:middle line:90%
on assuming those are all 0.

01:02:51.555 --> 01:02:53.430 align:middle line:84%
And then what's the
uncertainty in estimating

01:02:53.430 --> 01:02:56.010 align:middle line:90%
the rest of the coefficients?

01:02:56.010 --> 01:02:58.950 align:middle line:84%
This Weighted Likelihood
Bootstrap model

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:01.470 align:middle line:84%
is keeping track of both
uncertainty over which

01:03:01.470 --> 01:03:03.510 align:middle line:84%
coefficients are
nonzero and uncertainty

01:03:03.510 --> 01:03:04.960 align:middle line:90%
over the coefficient values.

01:03:04.960 --> 01:03:07.530 align:middle line:84%
So it's like giving you
a posterior over all

01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:09.600 align:middle line:84%
the different models
that might be true

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:12.970 align:middle line:84%
and reflecting that you don't
know what the correct model is.

01:03:12.970 --> 01:03:14.280 align:middle line:90%
You have a posterior over that.

01:03:14.280 --> 01:03:15.697 align:middle line:84%
And then you also
have a posterior

01:03:15.697 --> 01:03:17.610 align:middle line:84%
over coefficients
within the correct model

01:03:17.610 --> 01:03:21.383 align:middle line:84%
or within each correct
and incorrect model.

01:03:21.383 --> 01:03:22.800 align:middle line:84%
Typical way these
things are done,

01:03:22.800 --> 01:03:24.730 align:middle line:90%
you have a held-out sample.

01:03:24.730 --> 01:03:27.250 align:middle line:84%
You estimate the model
on 90% of the data,

01:03:27.250 --> 01:03:28.680 align:middle line:84%
and then see how
well it predicts

01:03:28.680 --> 01:03:30.490 align:middle line:90%
on the other 10% of the data.

01:03:30.490 --> 01:03:33.630 align:middle line:84%
And they say that from that
validation kind of exercise,

01:03:33.630 --> 01:03:37.560 align:middle line:84%
they find that the Weighted
Likelihood Bootstrap

01:03:37.560 --> 01:03:40.300 align:middle line:84%
estimates are the best estimates
relative to the other two.

01:03:40.300 --> 01:03:42.090 align:middle line:90%
That is, they do a--

01:03:42.090 --> 01:03:45.510 align:middle line:84%
the maximum likelihood
always fits the data best

01:03:45.510 --> 01:03:46.690 align:middle line:90%
on the in-sample part.

01:03:46.690 --> 01:03:48.572 align:middle line:84%
But then when you go
to the out-sample data,

01:03:48.572 --> 01:03:50.280 align:middle line:84%
this one is doing a
better job predicting

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:54.210 align:middle line:84%
the out-of-sample data
than are the MLE estimates.

01:03:54.210 --> 01:03:58.200 align:middle line:90%
So that's what they recommend.

01:03:58.200 --> 01:03:59.270 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

01:03:59.270 --> 01:04:03.770 align:middle line:84%
So first, this is really a
monopoly pricing problem--

01:04:03.770 --> 01:04:08.198 align:middle line:84%
a monopoly pricing
observation, not a price

01:04:08.198 --> 01:04:09.240 align:middle line:90%
discriminate observation.

01:04:09.240 --> 01:04:13.340 align:middle line:84%
But what they find is
that ZipRecruiter was not

01:04:13.340 --> 01:04:16.190 align:middle line:84%
short-run profit maximizing
to these consumers.

01:04:16.190 --> 01:04:19.610 align:middle line:84%
This chart here at the bottom
is, how much money would they

01:04:19.610 --> 01:04:20.903 align:middle line:90%
make?

01:04:20.903 --> 01:04:22.820 align:middle line:84%
How much money do they
make in the first month

01:04:22.820 --> 01:04:26.030 align:middle line:84%
off the customers who got
different prices shown to them?

01:04:26.030 --> 01:04:30.500 align:middle line:84%
And you show them $19 a
month, still only half of them

01:04:30.500 --> 01:04:31.820 align:middle line:90%
buy at $19 a month.

01:04:31.820 --> 01:04:35.000 align:middle line:84%
So you're only getting
like $8 a month in revenue.

01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:39.200 align:middle line:84%
As you go up, $39,
$59, $79, $99, you

01:04:39.200 --> 01:04:42.600 align:middle line:84%
keep making more money
because fewer people sign up,

01:04:42.600 --> 01:04:45.420 align:middle line:84%
but this price is
five times that price.

01:04:45.420 --> 01:04:47.300 align:middle line:84%
It's not one fifth the
number of customers.

01:04:47.300 --> 01:04:48.680 align:middle line:90%
You make more money.

01:04:48.680 --> 01:04:51.380 align:middle line:84%
But then you go to the
right of where ZipRecruiter

01:04:51.380 --> 01:04:55.280 align:middle line:84%
was at the time to
$159, $199 to $249,

01:04:55.280 --> 01:04:59.910 align:middle line:84%
and it looks like the profits
just keep marching up.

01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:04.110 align:middle line:84%
So the result is that
ZipRecruiter was just

01:05:04.110 --> 01:05:05.610 align:middle line:84%
charging too little
for its service

01:05:05.610 --> 01:05:08.470 align:middle line:84%
from the short-run profit
maximization perspective.

01:05:08.470 --> 01:05:12.000 align:middle line:84%
And the standard error
bars, they're not

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:14.020 align:middle line:90%
insignificant on each of these.

01:05:14.020 --> 01:05:16.920 align:middle line:84%
But clearly, if you're
fitting a curve through that,

01:05:16.920 --> 01:05:20.520 align:middle line:84%
it looks like it goes like this
and profits are maximized up

01:05:20.520 --> 01:05:23.240 align:middle line:90%
here somewhere.

01:05:23.240 --> 01:05:26.180 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, they estimate
the optimal price would be

01:05:26.180 --> 01:05:30.440 align:middle line:90%
$327 a month, not $99 a month.

01:05:30.440 --> 01:05:32.160 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:32.160 --> 01:05:32.660 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

01:05:32.660 --> 01:05:34.680 align:middle line:84%
So that's a test of
monopoly pricing,

01:05:34.680 --> 01:05:37.290 align:middle line:84%
is that ZipRecruiter is
not static monopoly pricing

01:05:37.290 --> 01:05:39.360 align:middle line:90%
before this experiment is run.

01:05:39.360 --> 01:05:41.820 align:middle line:84%
Incidentally, one of the
postscripts of the paper

01:05:41.820 --> 01:05:45.630 align:middle line:84%
is that seeing these results,
ZipRecruiter switched to $249

01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:48.090 align:middle line:90%
a month.

01:05:48.090 --> 01:05:50.460 align:middle line:84%
So then maybe short-run
profit maximization model

01:05:50.460 --> 01:05:52.740 align:middle line:84%
does work once you've
hired consultants

01:05:52.740 --> 01:05:55.590 align:middle line:90%
from Chicago Business School.

01:05:55.590 --> 01:05:58.620 align:middle line:84%
So second observation
is, there's substantial

01:05:58.620 --> 01:06:00.660 align:middle line:84%
heterogeneity, both
in price sensitivity

01:06:00.660 --> 01:06:02.500 align:middle line:90%
and in the consumer surplus.

01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:04.770 align:middle line:84%
So as I said, they've
got heterogeneity

01:06:04.770 --> 01:06:07.740 align:middle line:84%
both in the v's
and in the alphas.

01:06:07.740 --> 01:06:10.270 align:middle line:84%
And the paper says
there's a lot of both.

01:06:10.270 --> 01:06:15.110 align:middle line:84%
So this is the fitted
price coefficient,

01:06:15.110 --> 01:06:22.290 align:middle line:84%
what I was calling the Xi times
theta alpha, the predicted price

01:06:22.290 --> 01:06:23.640 align:middle line:90%
coefficient here.

01:06:23.640 --> 01:06:28.180 align:middle line:84%
And you can see, on an
absolute scale, this is 0.02.

01:06:28.180 --> 01:06:29.770 align:middle line:90%
So this is like 0.

01:06:29.770 --> 01:06:34.520 align:middle line:84%
So this thing is double the
price sensitivity of this.

01:06:34.520 --> 01:06:38.050 align:middle line:84%
There's just a great
deal of heterogeneity

01:06:38.050 --> 01:06:39.970 align:middle line:90%
in that price coefficient.

01:06:39.970 --> 01:06:42.130 align:middle line:84%
So there is substantial
number of people

01:06:42.130 --> 01:06:43.850 align:middle line:90%
who are-- it's around 0.3.

01:06:43.850 --> 01:06:46.703 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot who
it's around 0.6.

01:06:46.703 --> 01:06:49.120 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of difference
in what that price coefficient

01:06:49.120 --> 01:06:50.210 align:middle line:90%
seems to be.

01:06:50.210 --> 01:06:53.062 align:middle line:84%
And again, this exogenous
variation in price

01:06:53.062 --> 01:06:55.020 align:middle line:84%
means we can estimate
these things pretty well.

01:06:55.020 --> 01:06:57.610 align:middle line:90%


01:06:57.610 --> 01:06:59.410 align:middle line:84%
What is the
heterogeneity in how much

01:06:59.410 --> 01:07:02.908 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus those who
are purchasing are getting?

01:07:02.908 --> 01:07:04.450 align:middle line:84%
Again, their estimate
is that there's

01:07:04.450 --> 01:07:10.380 align:middle line:84%
this big upper tail of values
and that some people are getting

01:07:10.380 --> 01:07:12.880 align:middle line:84%
very little surplus and are on
the margin to buying and not,

01:07:12.880 --> 01:07:16.220 align:middle line:84%
but most people are getting
at least $50 worth of surplus.

01:07:16.220 --> 01:07:18.880 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
this thick fat tail

01:07:18.880 --> 01:07:20.590 align:middle line:84%
of people getting
very high consumer

01:07:20.590 --> 01:07:22.083 align:middle line:90%
surplus from this product.

01:07:22.083 --> 01:07:23.500 align:middle line:84%
So they're estimating
that there's

01:07:23.500 --> 01:07:26.920 align:middle line:84%
a tremendous amount of
consumer surplus, where

01:07:26.920 --> 01:07:27.880 align:middle line:90%
consumer means firm.

01:07:27.880 --> 01:07:33.310 align:middle line:84%
But firm surplus from using
ZipRecruiter at the time.

01:07:33.310 --> 01:07:34.880 align:middle line:90%
How much surplus is there?

01:07:34.880 --> 01:07:37.780 align:middle line:84%
Well, they estimate that
if you look at what optimal

01:07:37.780 --> 01:07:40.480 align:middle line:84%
personalized prices would be,
the optimal personalized prices

01:07:40.480 --> 01:07:47.440 align:middle line:84%
would range from $126
to $6,292 a month.

01:07:47.440 --> 01:07:50.433 align:middle line:84%
The $126 is noteworthy because
they were only charging $99.

01:07:50.433 --> 01:07:51.850 align:middle line:84%
So this would say,
even if someone

01:07:51.850 --> 01:07:53.470 align:middle line:84%
has all of the
attributes that make

01:07:53.470 --> 01:07:55.990 align:middle line:84%
you think they're the most
price-sensitive consumer

01:07:55.990 --> 01:07:58.650 align:middle line:84%
with the lowest value,
what would you charge?

01:07:58.650 --> 01:08:01.330 align:middle line:90%
The answer is $126.

01:08:01.330 --> 01:08:05.140 align:middle line:84%
$6,292, it's a little
heroic there because they

01:08:05.140 --> 01:08:07.760 align:middle line:84%
didn't experiment with
any price above $399.

01:08:07.760 --> 01:08:11.290 align:middle line:84%
So having an experiment where
the maximum price is $399

01:08:11.290 --> 01:08:13.180 align:middle line:84%
and then you say, OK,
given that experiment,

01:08:13.180 --> 01:08:15.580 align:middle line:84%
$6,000 is what you
should charge that guy,

01:08:15.580 --> 01:08:18.819 align:middle line:84%
obviously you've got all kinds
of functional form assumptions

01:08:18.819 --> 01:08:21.160 align:middle line:84%
for what happens out of sample,
and is that price really

01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:22.010 align:middle line:90%
correct?

01:08:22.010 --> 01:08:25.750 align:middle line:84%
So I think, in fact,
when they see things--

01:08:25.750 --> 01:08:30.010 align:middle line:84%
one thing they do say is, the
mean price would only be $277,

01:08:30.010 --> 01:08:32.510 align:middle line:84%
whereas its $327
with a uniform price.

01:08:32.510 --> 01:08:35.470 align:middle line:84%
So the skew is such that you
do charge a lot of people

01:08:35.470 --> 01:08:36.580 align:middle line:90%
low prices.

01:08:36.580 --> 01:08:41.290 align:middle line:84%
And even with the $6,000s mixed
in, you do a lower mean price

01:08:41.290 --> 01:08:45.850 align:middle line:84%
with discriminating than you
do without discriminating.

01:08:45.850 --> 01:08:50.620 align:middle line:84%
And the I think more reasonable
treatment of this data

01:08:50.620 --> 01:08:53.979 align:middle line:84%
is, let's look at-- suppose we
look at the personalized prices

01:08:53.979 --> 01:08:56.120 align:middle line:90%
and just cap them all at $499.

01:08:56.120 --> 01:08:57.670 align:middle line:90%
And when they actually--

01:08:57.670 --> 01:08:59.410 align:middle line:84%
ZipRecruiter said
that to them, is

01:08:59.410 --> 01:09:02.170 align:middle line:84%
that we're not going above
$499 no matter what you say.

01:09:02.170 --> 01:09:03.670 align:middle line:84%
So they have this
counterfactual of,

01:09:03.670 --> 01:09:05.620 align:middle line:84%
let's suppose they
discriminated,

01:09:05.620 --> 01:09:10.750 align:middle line:84%
but they let prices go up to
$499 based on this prediction.

01:09:10.750 --> 01:09:13.840 align:middle line:84%
The model estimates
were that discriminating

01:09:13.840 --> 01:09:19.029 align:middle line:84%
would increase profits by 8.2%
relative to the $327 price.

01:09:19.029 --> 01:09:23.720 align:middle line:84%
So if you discriminate, that's
the magnitude of discrimination.

01:09:23.720 --> 01:09:28.720 align:middle line:84%
So obviously it's much
bigger than Shiller's number.

01:09:28.720 --> 01:09:33.300 align:middle line:84%
But I think of it as
still relatively small.

01:09:33.300 --> 01:09:37.140 align:middle line:90%


01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:39.955 align:middle line:84%
What would the optimal
personalized prices do?

01:09:39.955 --> 01:09:42.330 align:middle line:84%
The optimal personalized prices
are the ones that include

01:09:42.330 --> 01:09:44.609 align:middle line:90%
the $6,000 numbers in them.

01:09:44.609 --> 01:09:46.620 align:middle line:84%
They say the optimal
personalized prices

01:09:46.620 --> 01:09:51.060 align:middle line:84%
would reduce consumer
surplus by about 25%

01:09:51.060 --> 01:09:55.410 align:middle line:84%
and would reduce social welfare
relative to the uniform pricing.

01:09:55.410 --> 01:09:56.260 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

01:09:56.260 --> 01:09:56.935 align:middle line:90%
Well, one thing.

01:09:56.935 --> 01:09:58.560 align:middle line:84%
The summary, essentially,
of the report

01:09:58.560 --> 01:10:01.200 align:middle line:84%
is, under these
discriminatory prices,

01:10:01.200 --> 01:10:04.710 align:middle line:84%
even though the mean is
$277 instead of $327,

01:10:04.710 --> 01:10:06.930 align:middle line:90%
demand goes down.

01:10:06.930 --> 01:10:08.400 align:middle line:84%
And the reason
is, they're taking

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:12.270 align:middle line:84%
all of these super high
value people and monopoly

01:10:12.270 --> 01:10:13.570 align:middle line:90%
pricing against them.

01:10:13.570 --> 01:10:18.840 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, when you
do monopoly pricing,

01:10:18.840 --> 01:10:21.360 align:middle line:84%
no matter how high demand
is, monopoly pricing,

01:10:21.360 --> 01:10:25.075 align:middle line:84%
you're always, in some sense,
selling to half the consumers.

01:10:25.075 --> 01:10:27.450 align:middle line:84%
And so if there are all these
high value people out there

01:10:27.450 --> 01:10:29.242 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay thousands
of dollars a month

01:10:29.242 --> 01:10:31.140 align:middle line:84%
and you still only
sell to half of them,

01:10:31.140 --> 01:10:33.270 align:middle line:84%
there's a huge amount
of deadweight loss

01:10:33.270 --> 01:10:34.980 align:middle line:90%
that comes from not selling to--

01:10:34.980 --> 01:10:38.910 align:middle line:84%
taking every one of those
really high value populations

01:10:38.910 --> 01:10:43.170 align:middle line:84%
and not selling to a
lot of people in those.

01:10:43.170 --> 01:10:45.960 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the estimate is
that consumer surplus goes down

01:10:45.960 --> 01:10:48.210 align:middle line:90%
by 25% here.

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:53.240 align:middle line:84%
And so total social
welfare has gone down.

01:10:53.240 --> 01:10:56.880 align:middle line:90%
Again, profits only go up by 8%.

01:10:56.880 --> 01:10:58.653 align:middle line:84%
Consumer surplus
goes down by 25%.

01:10:58.653 --> 01:11:01.070 align:middle line:84%
And then how big was consumer
surplus relative to profits?

01:11:01.070 --> 01:11:03.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, it looks like consumer
surplus was actually

01:11:03.200 --> 01:11:04.780 align:middle line:90%
quite big relative to profits.

01:11:04.780 --> 01:11:09.770 align:middle line:90%


01:11:09.770 --> 01:11:11.480 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:11:11.480 --> 01:11:13.850 align:middle line:84%
It's a nice paper in
that they actually

01:11:13.850 --> 01:11:17.060 align:middle line:84%
include my
Bergemann-Brooks-Morris triangle

01:11:17.060 --> 01:11:18.330 align:middle line:90%
themselves.

01:11:18.330 --> 01:11:19.980 align:middle line:84%
So I don't have to
draw in the arrow.

01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:23.010 align:middle line:84%
So here's what they say
personalization does.

01:11:23.010 --> 01:11:24.680 align:middle line:84%
So personalization
takes you from here,

01:11:24.680 --> 01:11:27.210 align:middle line:84%
and it's headed
in that direction.

01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:29.000 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to
the direction where

01:11:29.000 --> 01:11:30.770 align:middle line:90%
you're really getting only--

01:11:30.770 --> 01:11:32.840 align:middle line:90%
if this is the gap in there--

01:11:32.840 --> 01:11:34.230 align:middle line:84%
they've done this
for their data.

01:11:34.230 --> 01:11:36.597 align:middle line:84%
So this is the gap between
uninformed monopoly prices

01:11:36.597 --> 01:11:38.930 align:middle line:84%
and what they think would
happen with first degree price

01:11:38.930 --> 01:11:40.012 align:middle line:90%
discrimination.

01:11:40.012 --> 01:11:41.720 align:middle line:84%
And so first degree
price discrimination,

01:11:41.720 --> 01:11:43.950 align:middle line:84%
you'd make a huge
increase in profits.

01:11:43.950 --> 01:11:46.280 align:middle line:84%
You're only getting a
little bit off the floor

01:11:46.280 --> 01:11:49.730 align:middle line:84%
by doing this personalization
with 133 variables.

01:11:49.730 --> 01:11:52.280 align:middle line:84%
And you're getting it
in this arrow that's

01:11:52.280 --> 01:11:54.860 align:middle line:84%
going very much
horizontally, is their claim,

01:11:54.860 --> 01:11:58.280 align:middle line:84%
that consumer surplus is
just going down rapidly,

01:11:58.280 --> 01:12:02.430 align:middle line:84%
and profits is only going up
a little in this application.

01:12:02.430 --> 01:12:05.800 align:middle line:90%


01:12:05.800 --> 01:12:08.500 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, this
is one where this is--

01:12:08.500 --> 01:12:12.790 align:middle line:84%
it's a positive and negative
message from social perspective.

01:12:12.790 --> 01:12:16.520 align:middle line:84%
One is that it's
saying socially,

01:12:16.520 --> 01:12:19.970 align:middle line:84%
price discrimination is really
bad in this application.

01:12:19.970 --> 01:12:22.190 align:middle line:84%
It's destroying an awful
lot of social surplus

01:12:22.190 --> 01:12:24.210 align:middle line:84%
just to raise a
tiny bit of profit.

01:12:24.210 --> 01:12:26.120 align:middle line:84%
But then the positive
counterpoint to that

01:12:26.120 --> 01:12:28.562 align:middle line:84%
is that given the profit
gain is so little,

01:12:28.562 --> 01:12:30.020 align:middle line:84%
maybe the firm
wouldn't even bother

01:12:30.020 --> 01:12:33.120 align:middle line:84%
to do it because it's only
an 8% increase in profits.

01:12:33.120 --> 01:12:35.850 align:middle line:84%
And what are the other
downsides of doing it?

01:12:35.850 --> 01:12:39.230 align:middle line:84%
Are you going to pay the
consultants to do this and take

01:12:39.230 --> 01:12:42.110 align:middle line:84%
whatever political pressure,
bad news you might get,

01:12:42.110 --> 01:12:43.110 align:middle line:90%
or whatever to do it?

01:12:43.110 --> 01:12:44.990 align:middle line:84%
And in fact,
follow-up experiment

01:12:44.990 --> 01:12:47.840 align:middle line:84%
is, ZipRecruiter, in fact,
seeing these results,

01:12:47.840 --> 01:12:49.080 align:middle line:90%
did not do it.

01:12:49.080 --> 01:12:51.420 align:middle line:84%
ZipRecruiter said,
thank you very much.

01:12:51.420 --> 01:12:53.120 align:middle line:84%
Good to know our
prices are too low.

01:12:53.120 --> 01:12:55.520 align:middle line:90%
We'll start charging $249.

01:12:55.520 --> 01:12:57.530 align:middle line:84%
And actually, a very
nice part of the paper

01:12:57.530 --> 01:12:59.660 align:middle line:84%
is that ZipRecruiter then
let them come in and do

01:12:59.660 --> 01:13:03.590 align:middle line:84%
a follow-on study,
judging, you have

01:13:03.590 --> 01:13:05.300 align:middle line:84%
all these very
out-of-sample predictions

01:13:05.300 --> 01:13:06.660 align:middle line:90%
of what things would do.

01:13:06.660 --> 01:13:08.678 align:middle line:84%
Let's see you
discriminate in practice.

01:13:08.678 --> 01:13:09.720 align:middle line:90%
Again, a held-out sample.

01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:12.380 align:middle line:84%
So they use experiment
number one to predict--

01:13:12.380 --> 01:13:19.170 align:middle line:84%
to find the beta hat
or theta alpha hat.

01:13:19.170 --> 01:13:21.720 align:middle line:84%
And then that lets
them get, what

01:13:21.720 --> 01:13:24.180 align:middle line:90%
is pi hat for every consumer i?

01:13:24.180 --> 01:13:27.840 align:middle line:84%
Let's see how well does your
price discrimination scheme

01:13:27.840 --> 01:13:30.600 align:middle line:84%
work, reflecting
the fact that there

01:13:30.600 --> 01:13:32.220 align:middle line:84%
is estimation error
in these things

01:13:32.220 --> 01:13:34.262 align:middle line:84%
and you're trying it some
number of months later.

01:13:34.262 --> 01:13:36.030 align:middle line:84%
Does your model
still tell us who's

01:13:36.030 --> 01:13:38.100 align:middle line:90%
price sensitive or whatever?

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:40.360 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, they ran
a second experiment.

01:13:40.360 --> 01:13:45.100 align:middle line:84%
In the second experiment, $249
had been the standard price

01:13:45.100 --> 01:13:46.710 align:middle line:90%
before the experiment was run.

01:13:46.710 --> 01:13:49.470 align:middle line:84%
They experimented with
25% of the subjects

01:13:49.470 --> 01:13:55.560 align:middle line:84%
going back to the $99 price,
25% keeping the $249 price,

01:13:55.560 --> 01:14:00.090 align:middle line:84%
and then 50% of the sample being
on the personalized prices that

01:14:00.090 --> 01:14:05.670 align:middle line:90%
varied from $129 up to $499.

01:14:05.670 --> 01:14:08.370 align:middle line:84%
They rounded everything
to the nearest 9.

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:12.130 align:middle line:84%
And what you find is
that, really, it's

01:14:12.130 --> 01:14:16.205 align:middle line:84%
impressive in showing
that their model is pretty

01:14:16.205 --> 01:14:18.580 align:middle line:84%
accurately predicting what
would happen under these three

01:14:18.580 --> 01:14:19.640 align:middle line:90%
policies.

01:14:19.640 --> 01:14:22.990 align:middle line:84%
So what their prediction was
was that the mean probability

01:14:22.990 --> 01:14:28.540 align:middle line:84%
of signing up would be
26% in the $99 group,

01:14:28.540 --> 01:14:34.390 align:middle line:84%
15% in the $249 group, and only
14% in the personalized group

01:14:34.390 --> 01:14:36.550 align:middle line:84%
where they're charging
high prices to some

01:14:36.550 --> 01:14:38.770 align:middle line:90%
of these high value populations.

01:14:38.770 --> 01:14:41.920 align:middle line:84%
And what they find in the
data, just a simple comparison

01:14:41.920 --> 01:14:44.500 align:middle line:84%
of means, it was
actually only 23%

01:14:44.500 --> 01:14:48.190 align:middle line:84%
instead of 26% in the
$99 group, it matched 15%

01:14:48.190 --> 01:14:50.590 align:middle line:84%
in the $249 group,
and it was 15%

01:14:50.590 --> 01:14:53.280 align:middle line:84%
versus 14% in the
personalized group.

01:14:53.280 --> 01:14:55.230 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the
revenue predictions,

01:14:55.230 --> 01:14:58.470 align:middle line:84%
they were predicting the
revenues, per consumer,

01:14:58.470 --> 01:15:01.360 align:middle line:90%
would be $25, $38, $41.

01:15:01.360 --> 01:15:06.420 align:middle line:90%
It's $22, $38, $41.50.

01:15:06.420 --> 01:15:09.570 align:middle line:84%
So I think this experiment
gives you a lot of confidence

01:15:09.570 --> 01:15:13.980 align:middle line:84%
that, at least in the short
run, yes, price discrimination

01:15:13.980 --> 01:15:15.750 align:middle line:84%
would raise
ZipRecruiter's profits

01:15:15.750 --> 01:15:19.373 align:middle line:84%
by 8% using this feasible
price discrimination scheme.

01:15:19.373 --> 01:15:22.080 align:middle line:90%


01:15:22.080 --> 01:15:25.387 align:middle line:84%
And obviously,
ZipRecruiter, I believe,

01:15:25.387 --> 01:15:26.970 align:middle line:84%
having seen the
results of this, still

01:15:26.970 --> 01:15:29.730 align:middle line:84%
just stuck with the
$249 price and didn't

01:15:29.730 --> 01:15:32.220 align:middle line:84%
go ahead and change
and adopt this change

01:15:32.220 --> 01:15:34.145 align:middle line:84%
to try to get that
8% out of people.

01:15:34.145 --> 01:15:37.240 align:middle line:90%


01:15:37.240 --> 01:15:38.770 align:middle line:90%
OK, some comments.

01:15:38.770 --> 01:15:43.540 align:middle line:84%
Again, benefits of working
collaboratively with firms

01:15:43.540 --> 01:15:45.400 align:middle line:90%
is just huge here.

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:49.090 align:middle line:84%
It's very nice that Shiller
could be clever and think

01:15:49.090 --> 01:15:51.903 align:middle line:84%
about his identification
ability and the ability

01:15:51.903 --> 01:15:53.320 align:middle line:84%
to do this without
any price data,

01:15:53.320 --> 01:15:57.040 align:middle line:90%
but price variation is good.

01:15:57.040 --> 01:15:59.740 align:middle line:84%
So obviously, can you
walk into ZipRecruiter

01:15:59.740 --> 01:16:03.190 align:middle line:84%
and get them to run
this experiment for you?

01:16:03.190 --> 01:16:03.920 align:middle line:90%
Probably not.

01:16:03.920 --> 01:16:07.240 align:middle line:84%
But if you have friends who have
internet startups that are still

01:16:07.240 --> 01:16:09.490 align:middle line:84%
small enough they would
rather hire a graduate student

01:16:09.490 --> 01:16:12.460 align:middle line:84%
than some Chicago Business
School marketing professors

01:16:12.460 --> 01:16:15.070 align:middle line:84%
to do their
experiments for them,

01:16:15.070 --> 01:16:18.370 align:middle line:84%
finding those opportunities
to get data sets like this

01:16:18.370 --> 01:16:20.080 align:middle line:90%
and let someone run experiments.

01:16:20.080 --> 01:16:24.160 align:middle line:84%
And often, actually, you can
go to MIT alums or people

01:16:24.160 --> 01:16:30.700 align:middle line:84%
who work at not quite so
fancy companies may help out.

01:16:30.700 --> 01:16:32.585 align:middle line:90%
Wayfair is in Boston.

01:16:32.585 --> 01:16:34.460 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you have a friend
who works at Wayfair.

01:16:34.460 --> 01:16:37.760 align:middle line:84%
They can get you an introduction
to their data science team.

01:16:37.760 --> 01:16:41.780 align:middle line:84%
Could run experiments
through firms like that.

01:16:41.780 --> 01:16:46.220 align:middle line:84%
I have to say that
segmentation analysis here,

01:16:46.220 --> 01:16:48.140 align:middle line:84%
that second experiment
really shows you

01:16:48.140 --> 01:16:52.050 align:middle line:84%
that the Weighted
Likelihood Bootstrap Lasso

01:16:52.050 --> 01:16:54.450 align:middle line:84%
thing that they're doing
seems like that is doing

01:16:54.450 --> 01:16:56.040 align:middle line:84%
a good job of
segmenting the consumers

01:16:56.040 --> 01:16:57.248 align:middle line:90%
and predicting their demands.

01:16:57.248 --> 01:17:00.360 align:middle line:84%
And at least they don't give
you so much sense of like,

01:17:00.360 --> 01:17:01.920 align:middle line:84%
are the price
elasticities estimated

01:17:01.920 --> 01:17:04.503 align:middle line:84%
from the new data the same as
they would be from the old data?

01:17:04.503 --> 01:17:07.050 align:middle line:84%
But at least it looks like
this is a very good method

01:17:07.050 --> 01:17:08.470 align:middle line:90%
for segmenting people.

01:17:08.470 --> 01:17:13.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you wanted to think
about doing another study,

01:17:13.700 --> 01:17:16.880 align:middle line:84%
I think of the applied results
as being similar to Shiller's

01:17:16.880 --> 01:17:18.980 align:middle line:90%
in that demographics are just--

01:17:18.980 --> 01:17:21.200 align:middle line:84%
they have more demographics
than Shiller did.

01:17:21.200 --> 01:17:23.030 align:middle line:84%
They're using 133
features, whereas he

01:17:23.030 --> 01:17:25.400 align:middle line:90%
was using 33 features.

01:17:25.400 --> 01:17:27.192 align:middle line:84%
They're getting more
powerful results.

01:17:27.192 --> 01:17:29.150 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's a sense of
having done the demand

01:17:29.150 --> 01:17:30.470 align:middle line:90%
estimation better.

01:17:30.470 --> 01:17:32.993 align:middle line:90%
But it's still not all that big.

01:17:32.993 --> 01:17:36.123 align:middle line:90%


01:17:36.123 --> 01:17:38.540 align:middle line:84%
Another concern, as I said,
going back to Shiller's paper,

01:17:38.540 --> 01:17:42.530 align:middle line:84%
is it's a very nice
study, but it is--

01:17:42.530 --> 01:17:45.740 align:middle line:84%
got this maintained
assumptions that people

01:17:45.740 --> 01:17:48.770 align:middle line:84%
have these logit demands
with the logit demand errors,

01:17:48.770 --> 01:17:50.810 align:middle line:84%
and it's only heterogeneity
in this coefficient

01:17:50.810 --> 01:17:53.690 align:middle line:90%
and this coefficient.

01:17:53.690 --> 01:17:56.720 align:middle line:84%
Again, we would like
to know more about,

01:17:56.720 --> 01:17:58.820 align:middle line:84%
to what extent are
these predictions

01:17:58.820 --> 01:18:04.050 align:middle line:84%
of the model versus results
estimated from the model?

01:18:04.050 --> 01:18:06.050 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think it's nice
as an empirical result

01:18:06.050 --> 01:18:14.947 align:middle line:84%
that Shiller was finding this
and Dubé and Misra find this.

01:18:14.947 --> 01:18:17.030 align:middle line:84%
It makes you think that
maybe this model is fairly

01:18:17.030 --> 01:18:19.460 align:middle line:84%
flexible in which direction
those arrows could end up

01:18:19.460 --> 01:18:21.170 align:middle line:90%
pointing.

01:18:21.170 --> 01:18:25.580 align:middle line:84%
But it would be nice to
know more about that.

01:18:25.580 --> 01:18:27.590 align:middle line:84%
One other thing that's
important-- maybe this

01:18:27.590 --> 01:18:30.860 align:middle line:84%
is more of a practical
consulting point than a paper.

01:18:30.860 --> 01:18:37.120 align:middle line:84%
But these experiments
only identify

01:18:37.120 --> 01:18:39.440 align:middle line:90%
short-run individual effects.

01:18:39.440 --> 01:18:41.440 align:middle line:84%
They have a bit in the
paper where they look at,

01:18:41.440 --> 01:18:43.910 align:middle line:84%
do people persist on
ZipRecruiter for more than just

01:18:43.910 --> 01:18:47.560 align:middle line:84%
the sign-up month if you charge
them $249 instead of $99?

01:18:47.560 --> 01:18:49.630 align:middle line:84%
But there are all
kinds of things

01:18:49.630 --> 01:18:51.220 align:middle line:84%
that these estimates
are never going

01:18:51.220 --> 01:18:55.990 align:middle line:84%
to-- these sort of what we call
A/B testing in models like this

01:18:55.990 --> 01:18:57.700 align:middle line:84%
are going to not
tell you, which is,

01:18:57.700 --> 01:19:01.030 align:middle line:84%
what are the general
equilibrium or systemic effects?

01:19:01.030 --> 01:19:03.550 align:middle line:84%
Is there a word of mouth
customer acquisition effect,

01:19:03.550 --> 01:19:06.550 align:middle line:84%
where someone signs up
for ZipRecruiter at $99,

01:19:06.550 --> 01:19:08.050 align:middle line:84%
they're satisfied
with ZipRecruiter,

01:19:08.050 --> 01:19:09.890 align:middle line:84%
they recommend ZipRecruiter
to someone else.

01:19:09.890 --> 01:19:12.610 align:middle line:84%
Whereas the $249 people are
signing up for ZipRecruiter,

01:19:12.610 --> 01:19:16.660 align:middle line:84%
but they're not telling their
friends to sign up for it.

01:19:16.660 --> 01:19:20.170 align:middle line:84%
Does the price
per quality affect

01:19:20.170 --> 01:19:22.060 align:middle line:84%
when the experts
are writing articles

01:19:22.060 --> 01:19:27.610 align:middle line:84%
in the newspaper about which
employment site should your firm

01:19:27.610 --> 01:19:29.350 align:middle line:90%
work with.

01:19:29.350 --> 01:19:32.710 align:middle line:84%
Is there some possibility that
there will be popular outrage?

01:19:32.710 --> 01:19:37.950 align:middle line:84%
Also, we've dropped the
number of firms here.

01:19:37.950 --> 01:19:45.080 align:middle line:84%
It was 23% going down
to 15% using the site.

01:19:45.080 --> 01:19:47.330 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, that means
there are one third fewer

01:19:47.330 --> 01:19:50.960 align:middle line:84%
jobs on the site from
small new employers.

01:19:50.960 --> 01:19:53.780 align:middle line:84%
One third fewer jobs is
going to mean fewer resumes

01:19:53.780 --> 01:19:54.840 align:middle line:90%
posted to the site.

01:19:54.840 --> 01:19:56.520 align:middle line:84%
Fewer resumes
posted to the site,

01:19:56.520 --> 01:19:59.030 align:middle line:84%
is the thing less
valuable to employers?

01:19:59.030 --> 01:20:04.250 align:middle line:84%
Is there a spiral down where
fewer people sign up for it,

01:20:04.250 --> 01:20:07.340 align:middle line:84%
therefore fewer
people use the site?

01:20:07.340 --> 01:20:08.870 align:middle line:90%
Thinking about it.

01:20:08.870 --> 01:20:10.250 align:middle line:84%
Also, I think John
is going to do

01:20:10.250 --> 01:20:13.290 align:middle line:84%
in a few weeks
two-sided market models.

01:20:13.290 --> 01:20:15.110 align:middle line:84%
But this is a
two-sided market model

01:20:15.110 --> 01:20:17.610 align:middle line:84%
where you shouldn't be doing
the one-sided monopoly pricing.

01:20:17.610 --> 01:20:20.172 align:middle line:84%
You should think about,
we're setting our price.

01:20:20.172 --> 01:20:22.880 align:middle line:84%
It's a multi-product thing where
I might price to firms and price

01:20:22.880 --> 01:20:24.090 align:middle line:90%
to consumers.

01:20:24.090 --> 01:20:25.728 align:middle line:84%
My price to consumers
is fixed at zero,

01:20:25.728 --> 01:20:27.770 align:middle line:84%
but I still want more of
them to be there to make

01:20:27.770 --> 01:20:29.400 align:middle line:90%
it more valuable for the firms.

01:20:29.400 --> 01:20:35.290 align:middle line:84%
I should be thinking that
multi-side thing into effect.

01:20:35.290 --> 01:20:38.530 align:middle line:84%
And actually, final
thing is, this

01:20:38.530 --> 01:20:40.570 align:middle line:84%
is a feature from
several years ago

01:20:40.570 --> 01:20:45.700 align:middle line:84%
where Jeff Bezos, back in the
early days of Amazon, Amazon

01:20:45.700 --> 01:20:47.920 align:middle line:84%
appeared to be price
discriminating against consumers

01:20:47.920 --> 01:20:50.560 align:middle line:84%
and charging different
people different amounts,

01:20:50.560 --> 01:20:52.540 align:middle line:84%
just like there's
outrage against price

01:20:52.540 --> 01:20:56.980 align:middle line:84%
gouging for gasoline
during bad weather.

01:20:56.980 --> 01:20:59.685 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, Amazon get into
a tremendous bad press

01:20:59.685 --> 01:21:01.060 align:middle line:84%
for discriminating
against people

01:21:01.060 --> 01:21:04.150 align:middle line:84%
and charging some
people more than others.

01:21:04.150 --> 01:21:07.000 align:middle line:84%
And Amazon had to come
out and say, OK, we

01:21:07.000 --> 01:21:08.770 align:middle line:84%
promise we're never
going to do this.

01:21:08.770 --> 01:21:12.460 align:middle line:84%
Again, we don't know, are there
going to be similar effects--

01:21:12.460 --> 01:21:15.053 align:middle line:84%
is this part of why ZipRecruiter
said 8% is not enough,

01:21:15.053 --> 01:21:17.470 align:middle line:84%
because they don't want to get
caught price discriminating

01:21:17.470 --> 01:21:19.750 align:middle line:90%
against people?

01:21:19.750 --> 01:21:20.350 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:21:20.350 --> 01:21:21.980 align:middle line:90%
That's what I've got for today.

01:21:21.980 --> 01:21:26.140 align:middle line:84%
So Wednesday, I'm back
to oligopoly competition.

01:21:26.140 --> 01:21:28.570 align:middle line:90%
It's also going to be a very--

01:21:28.570 --> 01:21:31.140 align:middle line:84%
back to a mostly textbook
chapter where a lot of it

01:21:31.140 --> 01:21:31.640 align:middle line:90%
is old.

01:21:31.640 --> 01:21:33.223 align:middle line:84%
I'll get to some new
stuff at the end.

01:21:33.223 --> 01:21:34.900 align:middle line:84%
But I think actually
the new theory

01:21:34.900 --> 01:21:36.800 align:middle line:84%
gets pushed into
next Monday's lecture

01:21:36.800 --> 01:21:39.860 align:middle line:84%
because I'm doing less
empirical and more theory

01:21:39.860 --> 01:21:42.010 align:middle line:90%
on oligopoly pricing.

01:21:42.010 --> 01:22:00.000 align:middle line:90%